\par\par \b \uc2 Bar For Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Interventions \highlight\uc2  Seen as Very High -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,555 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2022\par 15:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0931 ET - The bar looks "very, very high" for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to use \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curtail the yen's depreciation, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. That is because \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at bolstering a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are frowned upon by G7 countries, \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Ulrich Leuchtmann\uc2  says in a note. "I think that for the time being the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will try verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and will sound continuously more concerned about the yen-weakness," he says. Japanese policymakers will take this approach in the hope that it will end the yen's collapse for fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he says. USD/JPY rises to 128.740, its highest level since May 2002, according to Refinitiv. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 ET - Euro investment-grade non-financial corporate bond spreads have tightened to pre-Ukraine war levels and ING now sees a risk of them widening. Euro IG credit spreads sit at the bottom end of the Dutch bank's defined range of 58-84 basis points, having fully retraced from the wide levels seen in early March at the start of Russia's invasion of Ukraine, ING says in a research note. "There is a real possibility that spreads will widen from these levels, on the back of continued uncertainties around the Russia-Ukraine conflict, [the] \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ending [the] Corporate Sector Purchase Program [and] inflation and supply indigestion," says ING's credit research team. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 ET - Some elements of French far-right presidential candidate \uc2 Marine Le Pen\uc2  's election manifesto, such as reintroducing border controls or slashing France's financing contributions to the EU, have "almost no chance" of getting majority approval by EU states, says \uc2 Edmond de Rothschild Asset Management\uc2  . Le Pen 's push to end the primacy of EU law over French law is another policy that would struggle to receive approval, says global chief investment officer Benjamin Melman. Hungary and Poland are the most sympathetic to reinstating the primacy of national over EU law but the most opposed to cuts in financial contributions to Brussels. "In this particular case, European institutions, which are the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bedrock, could be paralysed," Melman says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0904 ET - Volatility is set to remain elevated in government bond markets after \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  official \uc2 James Bullard\uc2  said Monday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could raise interest rates more aggressively, says \uc2 Saxo Bank\uc2  in a research note. "He planted the seeds of a 75 basis-point rate hike given that the Fed needs to get to neutral rate very soon," says Althea Spinozzi, senior fixed income strategist. Saxo's base case for the May meeting is still a 50-basis-point rate increase, though "a final word on that should be watched from Fed Chair Powell on Thursday as he speaks at the \uc2 IMF\uc2  conference." Bond yields rise, with the yield on the 10-year U.K. benchmark government bond trading last at 1.967%, having earlier hit an intraday high of 1.994%, the highest since Jan. 2016, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0902 ET - Rising interest rates in the U.S. are set to temper demand for housing but the market still has strong underlying fundamentals, RCLCO Real Estate Consulting's principal Kelly Mangold says. "Even with rising interest rates and issues surrounding geopolitical stability, supply-chain issues, and inflation, the overall lack of inventory over the past year has continued to drive demand for more housing starts as builders continue to try to push inventory to market," she says. Demographics continue to drive demand for home ownership, but the competition for homes could decline in the months ahead, putting the market closer to equilibrium, Mangold says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0757 ET - The euro is likely to fall versus the dollar over the coming year as central banks tighten monetary policy to stem inflation despite a slowdown in global manufacturing, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says. "Global manufacturing is slowing, valuations shows risks are to the downside for spot [EUR/USD] and the near-term consequences of the war in Ukraine will likely be a further strengthening of the USD," \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  analyst Lars Merklin says in a note. Meanwhile, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will act in a way that ensures inflation eases over the coming year whereas the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise interest rates moderately, he says. \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  expects EUR/USD to fall to 1.05 in 12 months, from 1.0796 currently. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0750 ET - Gilt traders sell gilts heavily Tuesday as they return to their desks for the first time since Thursday due to public holidays on Friday and Monday. The yield on the U.K. benchmark 10-year government bond, or gilt, rises almost 10 basis points on the day to 1.994%, its highest since January 2016, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . Bond traders are reacting to more aggressive rhetoric from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  as expectations of high inflation and interest-rate increases intensify, pushing the 10-year Treasury yield to an intraday high of 2.907%, the highest since November 2018. But buyers arose at those levels, taking the 10-year gilt yield and the 10-year Treasury yield down to 1.975% and 2.887% respectively, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . Yields move inversely to bond prices. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0734 ET - Investors pulled money out of corporate bond funds in the week ended April 13, snapping a two-week streak of inflows, says \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  citing data from fund-tracker EPFR. "The inflow trend into risk assets did not last too long," analysts at the U.S. bank say in a research note. The combination of geopolitical risks and hawkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric has weighed on risk assets, including emerging market funds as well as equity funds, which have both suffered outflows during the same period. They says macroeconomic data will continue to deteriorate, which coupled with elevated rates-market uncertainty and unresolved geopolitical risks indicate flows into risk assets will struggle. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0732 ET - \uc2 SEB\uc2  expects the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  board to leave the repo rate unchanged at next week's meeting but signal a high probability for a hike at the June meeting. \uc2 SEB\uc2  sticks to its forecast that quantitative easing purchases will be lowered to SEK20 billion/quarter in the second half of the year, from SEK37 billion/quarter. Higher than expected inflation and hawkish signals in \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  speeches mean the Swedish financial group now predicts a rate hike in June and two more at the meetings in September and November, with another two in 2023 taking the repo rate to 1.25% in the middle of next year. \uc2 SEB\uc2  previously saw a first rate hike in November and three more to 1.0% at the end of 2023. (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0724 ET - The Norwegian krone is likely to weaken versus the euro on rising global recession risks but only in the near-term, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says. "Near-term we still think risks are skewed to the topside [for EUR/NOK] amid rising global recession risks as central banks rapidly tighten policy at a time of supply shocks (Covid-19, war in Ukraine)," \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  analyst Kristoffer Kjaer Lomholt says in a note. However, over the longer term, EUR/NOK should fall given the outlook for elevated inflation and commodity prices in the years ahead, he says. \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  expects EUR/NOK trade at 10.00 in six months and 9.80 in 12 months, compared to the current level of 9.5446. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0723 ET - The \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  looks set to start raising interest rates soon but the Swedish krona's gains will be limited given the market has already anticipated aggressive rate rises, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says. Following recent remarks from \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  policymakers about the prospect of an earlier rate rise, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  analysts say they now expect the first increase in June compared to September previously. "Such an outcome should be relatively neutral (slightly positive) for SEK," they say. In the medium-term, a bleaker Swedish growth outlook and risk of U.S/ global recession could restrict the krona's appreciation potential, they say. \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  expects EUR/SEK to fall to 10.10 in six months before rising to 10.20 in 12 months. EUR/SEK is last down 0.1% at 10.3370. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 ET - Companies are facing a challenging business environment amid the Ukraine war and stringent lockdowns across China, which could weigh on their earnings forecasts and limit the scope for credit-rating upgrades, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. "While aggregated earnings expectations have held up well so far...this reporting season could hence mark a turning point for fundamentals and could set the stage for guidance cuts beyond what has been already factored in by analysts," says Marco Stoeckle, head of corporate credit research at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  , in a research note. This suggests "scope for rating upgrades could evaporate swiftly and further weaken the investment case for credit, particularly for lockdown affected sectors and lower-rated segments," he says. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 19, 2022 09:31 ET (13:31 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220419ei4j0035l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
