\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar index passes 101 for first time in two years as yen slide continues\b0\par\par\uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2022\par 07:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Dollar gains another 0.9% on yen\par \par \uc2 * Japanese finance minister warns of risks from yen slump\par \par \uc2 * Dollar hits one year high on Swiss franc\par \par \uc2 * Aussie boosted by \uc2 RBA\uc2  minutes showing rate hike getting closer\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 By Alun John\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, April 19 (Reuters) - The dollar index rose past 101 for the first time since March 2020 on Tuesday, as the greenback set its latest 20-year high on the yen and tested a two-year peak on the euro, supported by high \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields and expectations of good economic data.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which measures the greenback against six peers, was last at 100.99, up 0.2%, having risen as high as 101.02 in early trade. It has gained 2.6% so far this month.\par \par \uc2 "I think the broad dollar trend reflects U.S. economic outperformance, while we've seen some initial impacts of higher energy prices from the Ukraine war elsewhere, especially in the euro zone," said Carol Kong, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She added that she was watching purchasing manager index data due in several markets on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "If we get weak PMI numbers in the euro zone or elsewhere, then markets could potentially downgrade their expectations for the global economy, but I don't think the U.S. PMI will be particularly weak, so we'll see some contrast there, which would probably support the dollar," she said.\par \par \uc2 "Of course, the big driver for dollar-yen has been surging U.S. bond yields."\par \par \uc2 The dollar's gains have been most striking against the against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and on Tuesday it climbed 0.73% to 127.88 yen, its highest level since May 2002.\par \par \uc2 It has risen 4.5% on the yen so far this month, which would be its second-biggest monthly percentage gain since 2016 behind last month's 5.8%.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark U.S. 10-year Treasury yield on Tuesday was at 2.8376 hovering just off its three-year high of 2.884% hit Monday, while the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yield on Japanese 10 year government bonds around 0% and no higher than 0.25%.\par \par \uc2 Many investors are betting the yen has further to fall. The latest CFTC data for the week ending April 12 shows net short yen positions are the largest in three and a half years.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  said on Tuesday the damage to the economy from a weakening yen at present is greater than the benefits from it, the most explicit warning against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent slump.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the dollar rose to as high as 0.9466 Swiss francs , its highest in a year, and was also testing peaks against other majors.\par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.0776, just off last week's two-year low of $1.0756, and sterling was at $1.3009, in sight of its 18 month low against the dollar of $1.2973, also hit last week.\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weren't helped by the latest fighting in Ukraine, which said Russia had started an anticipated new offensive in the east of the country.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar rose 0.3% from Monday's one-month low and was at $0.737, given some support by minutes published Tuesday from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 's April policy meeting, which suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was edging closer to raising interest rates for the first time in more than a decade due to accelerating inflation.\par \par \uc2 Bitcoin also managed to find its feet, trading around $40,800 on Tuesday after hitting a one-month low of $38,547 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Alun John; Editing by Stephen Coates and Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-4-19T06:17:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220419ei4j00gwd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
