S$             76.12        0.31\par  MYR US$         4.231          0.002       TWD US$             29.145       0.095\par  CNY US$         6.3663         -0.0032     HKD US$             7.8372       -0.0009\par  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par --     \par  Commodities                                                                 \par  Spot Gold       1,968.4141     14.9817     Silver (Lon)        25.3936      0.3196\par  U.S. Gold Fut   1,973          5.7         Brent Crude         104.95       6.47\par  Iron Ore        CNY925         56          TRJCRB Index        -            -\par  TOCOM Rubber    JPY261.6       -1.2        \f28 \uc2 \f2 LME\f28 \uc2 \f2  Copper          10,342       165\par  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par --  \par     ** indicates closing price \par     All prices as of 18:29 GMT\par     \par     EQUITIES\par \par     GLOBAL - World stocks edged higher after a wobbly start on Tuesday, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S.\par Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields slipped as data showing a jump in U.S. inflation was in line with\par expectations.\par     \par     MSCI's gauge of stocks across the globe was up 0.44%.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     NEW YORK - The Nasdaq led the charge on Wall Street on Tuesday after data showed\par inflation rose largely in line with estimates, lifting growth stocks that were hammered\par by a surge in Treasury yields on expectations of aggressive U.S. interest rate hikes.\par     \par     At 12:02 a.m. ET, the Dow Jones Industrial Average was up 235.26 points, or\par 0.69%, at 34,543.34, the S&P 500 was up 38.90 points, or 0.88%, at 4,451.43, and\par the Nasdaq Composite was up 162.29 points, or 1.21%, at 13,574.24.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - European shares fell on Tuesday as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  slumped\par after a big stake sale, while a U.S. reading on inflation kept aggressive \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal\par Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  tightening bets from ramping up.\par     \par     The pan-European STOXX 600 index fell 0.3%, paring some losses from\par earlier in the day, with healthcare stocks leading losses, and banks .SX7P\par among the worst hit.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - Japanese shares closed lower on Tuesday to their lowest in nearly four\par weeks as heavyweight tech stocks led the decline after they tracked a sharp overnight\par drop on Wall Street.\par     \par     The Nikkei share average ended 1.81% lower at 26,334.98, the lowest since\par March 16. The broader Topix fell 1.38% to end at 1,863.63.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI -  China stocks rebounded in afternoon trading to close higher on Tuesday,\par as hopes of easing in COVID-19 curbs in some pilot areas lifted tourism and consumer\par goods sectors, while expectations of policy support for the economy lifted sentiment.\par     \par     The blue-chip CSI300 index ended 2.0% higher at 4,179.97, while the\par Shanghai Composite Index gained 1.5% to 3,213.33 points.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - Australian shares closed lower on Tuesday, in line with broader Asia as\par investors awaited U.S. inflation data that is expected to reinforce the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal\par Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's stance of aggressive policy normalisation to contain broadening pricing\par pressure.\par     \par     The S&P/ASX 200 index slipped 0.4% to close at 7,454.0, after earlier\par hitting a two-week low of 7,430.80, with all sectors except gold in negative territory.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - South Korean shares fell 1% on Tuesday to their lowest close in nearly a\par month amid cautious mood ahead of the U.S. inflation data. The Korean won weakened,\par while the benchmark bond yield rose.\par     The benchmark KOSPI ended down 26.34 points, or 0.98%, to 2,666.76, marking\par the lowest close since March 16.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par     NEW YORK - The dollar slid on Tuesday after U.S. inflation data showed consumer\par prices rose by 8.5% in March compared to a year ago, boosted by soaring gasoline, but\par tempered by less expensive used cars and trucks.\par     \par     The dollar index fell 0.146%, with the euro down 0.02% to $1.0881.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI - The yuan weakened slightly on Tuesday on a softer daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par fixing and as the U.S. dollar firmed ahead of data that is expected to show the fastest\par rise in inflation in more than 16 years.\par     \par     Prior to the market open, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set yuan's daily midpoint\par  at 6.3795 per dollar, its weakest fixing since April 6.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - The Australian and New Zealand dollars were in retreat on Tuesday as\par U.S. bond yields rose ever higher and shook sentiment in risk assets, with investors\par increasingly concerned policy tightening will slow the global economy.\par     \par     The Aussie was down for a fifth straight session at $0.7408 leaving last\par week's 10-month top of $0.7661 a distant memory. Support comes in around $0.7360 and\par the 200-day moving average at $0.7297.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - In offshore trading, the won was quoted at 1,233.0 per dollar on\par Tuesday, up 0.1% from the previous day, while in non-deliverable forward trading its\par one-month contract KRW1MNDFOR= was quoted at 1,232.9.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TREASURIES\par     \par     NEW YORK - \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields moved lower on Tuesday, with the benchmark 10-year\par Treasury yield on track for its first decline in eight sessions after a reading on\par inflation showed an acceleration in March, but was less than many market participants\par expected.\par     \par     The yield on 10-year Treasury notes was down 6.6 basis points to\par 2.716%. The yield had earlier climbed to 2.836%, its highest since Dec. 19, 2018.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - Euro zone government bond yields fell on Tuesday, tracking moves in U.S.\par Treasuries after U.S. inflation data.\par     \par     The French 10-year government bond yield rose 2.5 bps to 1.359%, with the spread to\par its German peer widening 1 bps to 48.8.,\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - Japan's 10-year government bonds were flat on Tuesday, even as their U.S.\par peers hit multi-year year highs, as traders remained cautious about the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     \par     The 10-year JGB yield was unchanged at 0.235%.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par  \par     \par     COMMODITIES\par     \par     GOLD\par \par     Gold advanced more than 1% on Tuesday as Treasury yields eased after U.S. inflation\par data largely met expectations, reducing the likelihood of long-term aggressive policy\par tightening by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     \par     Spot gold was up rose 0.8% to $1,968.51 per ounce by 2:16 p.m. ET (1816\par GMT), hitting its highest in nearly a month earlier. U.S. gold futures rose 1.3%\par to $1,973.00.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     IRON ORE\par     \par     Chinese steel futures bounced back on Tuesday after posting big losses in the\par previous session, as the state council urged local governments to ensure smooth\par transportation of key production materials.\par     \par     The most-active steel rebar on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shanghai Futures Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 , for October\par delivery, jumped 2.2% to 5,035 yuan ($790.57) per tonne at close.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     BASE METALS\par     \par     Zinc prices climbed on Tuesday to their highest in more than a week as dwindling\par inventories and shortages, particularly in Europe where record-high power prices have\par led to large output cuts, spurred buying.\par     \par     Benchmark zinc on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Metal Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  was up 2.6% at $4,400 a tonne\par at 1615 GMT from an earlier $4,405.50, the highest since April 4. Prices of the metal,\par used to galvanise steel, hit a record high of $4,896 a tonne last month.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     OIL\par     \par     Oil prices climbed on Tuesday as Russian oil and gas condensate production fell to\par 2020 lows and \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  warned it would be impossible to replace potential supply losses\par from Russia.\par     \par     Brent crude futures rose by $5.74, or 5.8%, to $104.22 a barrel by 1:48\par p.m. EDT (1748 GMT). U.S. West Texas Intermediate was up $5.90, or 6.3%, at\par $100.19. On Monday, both benchmarks fell about 4%.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     PALM OIL\par     \par     Malaysian palm oil futures climbed on Tuesday to their highest in nearly three\par weeks, buoyed by tight inventories even as analysts forecast a drop in April exports.\par     \par     The benchmark palm oil contract for June delivery on the Bursa Malaysia\par Derivatives Exchange rose 175 ringgit, or 2.91%, to 6,180 ringgit ($1,460.65) a tonne,\par extending gains for a third session and hitting its highest closing price since March\par 23.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     RUBBER\par     \par     Japanese rubber futures inched lower on Tuesday as the Nikkei weakened, although a\par softer yen and tight raw material supply limited losses.\par     \par     The Osaka Exchange rubber contract for September delivery,\par finished 0.4 yen, or 0.2 %, lower at 262.8 yen ($2.09) per kg.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par \par  (Bengaluru Bureau; +91 80 6749 1130)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-4-12T19:30:12.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220412ei4c039o5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
