4,365         5           INR US$             75.8294      -0.1087\par  MYR US$         4.229          0.009       TWD US$             29.05        0.144\par  CNY US$         6.3695         0.0051      HKD US$             7.8376       -0.0011\par  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par --     \par  Commodities                                                                 \par  Spot Gold       1,953.1417     7.2917      Silver (Lon)        25.0646      0.3146\par  U.S. Gold Fut   1,956.40       10.8        Brent Crude         99.04        -3.74\par  Iron Ore        CNY869         -50         TRJCRB Index        -            -\par  TOCOM Rubber    JPY262.2       -1          \f28 \uc2 \f2 LME\f28 \uc2 \f2  Copper          10,177       -158\par  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par --  \par     ** indicates closing price \par     All prices as of 20:39 GMT\par  \par     EQUITIES\par     GLOBAL -  A gauge of global stocks skidded on Monday, pulled lower by technology\par shares, as \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields marched higher ahead of inflation data that could\par prompt the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  to tighten policy enough to slow a rebounding economy.\par     \par     MSCI's gauge of stocks across the globe shed 1.01%, while the\par pan-European STOXX 600 index lost 0.61%.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     NEW YORK - The S&P and the Nasdaq fell more than 1% on Monday as rising bond yields\par weighed on megacap growth stocks such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Microsoft\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alphabet\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Apple\f28 \uc2 \f2 , with investors\par on edge ahead of Tuesday's inflation data.\par     \par     At 12:45 p.m. ET, the Dow Jones Industrial Average was down 235.40 points,\par or 0.68%, at 34,485.72, the S&P 500 was down 56.89 points, or 1.27%, at\par 4,431.39, and the Nasdaq Composite was down 239.36 points, or 1.75%, at\par 13,471.63.\par     \par      For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - European stocks fell on Monday as surging bond yields hit technology\par shares and volatility gripped French blue-chip stocks on forecasts of a tight race\par between President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Emmanuel Macron\f28 \uc2 \f2  and far-right challenger \f28 \uc2 \f2 Marine Le Pen\f28 \uc2 \f2  in the final\par round of the election.\par     \par     The pan-European STOXX 600 index dropped 0.6%, led by a 2.2% slide in\par technology stocks. Among the regional indexes, Germany's DAX slipped\par 0.6% and the UK's FTSE 100 .FTSE fell 0.7%.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - Japan's Nikkei ended down on Monday, as heavyweight technology stocks\par tracked the Nasdaq lower, hit by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's aggressive move to tackle\par inflation.\par     \par     The Nikkei share average lost 0.61% to close at 26,821.52, after trading\par higher earlier in the session. The broader Topix slipped 0.38% to 1,889.64,\par also turning positive briefly.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI - Chinese shares posted their biggest drop in nearly a month on Monday hit\par by COVID-19 curbs and worries over an inversion in the 10-year spread between domestic\par and U.S. yields, high domestic factory-gate and consumer inflation.\par     \par      The blue-chip CSI300 index fell 3.1% to 4,100.07, and the Shanghai\par Composite Index lost 2.6% to 3,167.13.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - Australian shares are expected to drop at the open on Tuesday, tracking\par a weaker overnight session on Wall Street, while dull commodity prices are also\par expected to weigh on the resource-heavy bourse.\par     The local share price index futures fell 0.4%, a 50.2-point discount to\par the underlying S&P/ASX 200 index close. The benchmark rose 0.1% on Monday.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - In the stock market, the benchmark KOSPI closed down 7.29 points,\par or 0.27%, at 2,693.10 as China's faster-than-expected inflation data added to inflation\par concerns.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     NEW YORK - The dollar held just below two-year highs against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par on Monday as it continued to be supported by higher \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields, while the\par euro rallied after French leader \f28 \uc2 \f2 Emmanuel Macron\f28 \uc2 \f2  beat far-right challenger Marine Le\par Pen in the country's first round of presidential voting.\par     \par     The U.S. dollar index was last at 99.94, after reaching 100.19 on Friday,\par the highest since May 2020.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI -  China's yuan weakened against the dollar on Monday, dragged lower by\par investor concern over capital outflow and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation pressure after\par benchmark yield differentials between the world's two largest economies turned\par negative.\par     \par     In the spot market, onshore yuan opened at 6.3650 per dollar and was\par changing hands at 6.3725 at midday, 81 pips weaker than the previous late session\par close.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA -  The Australian and New Zealand dollars were on the defensive on Monday\par as yield spreads continued to widen in favour of their U.S. counterpart, and investors\par waited to see how aggressive \f28 \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be on rates this week.\par     \par     The Aussie eased to $0.7447 and away from last week's 10-month top of\par $0.7661. Support comes in around $0.7425 and $0.7360.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - The won closed trading at 1,233.1 per dollar on the onshore settlement\par platform on Monday, 0.65% lower than its previous close at 1,225.1.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TREASURIES\par     \par     NEW YORK - The benchmark 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yield rose on Monday to its highest\par level in more than three years as investors awaited key inflation data later this week\par to determine how hawkish the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  will need to be on its policy path.\par     \par     The yield on 10-year Treasury notes was up 3.8 basis points to 2.753%\par after climbing to 2.784%, its highest level since January 2019. The yield was on track\par to climb for a seventh straight session.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - Spreads between French and \f28 \uc2 \f2 German government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond yields tightened on\par Monday after French leader \f28 \uc2 \f2 Emmanuel Macron\f28 \uc2 \f2  came out with the biggest proportion of the\par vote in the first round of the presidential election.\par     \par     France's 10-year government bond yield rose 1.5 bps to 1.288%, after briefly\par hitting its highest since July 2015 at 1.309% earlier in the session.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - Japanese government bond (JGB) yields rose on Monday, tracking \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S.\par Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields higher, although caution around the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's possible\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend its target limited the sell-off.\par     \par     The 10-year JGB yield rose one basis point to 0.235%.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par  \par     \par     COMMODITIES\par     \par     GOLD\par      Palladium prices hit a more than two-week high on Monday on supply concerns\par following a recent suspension on trading of the metal sourced from Russia in the London\par hub, while gold was boosted by inflation fears.\par     \par     Palladium was up 0.7% at $2,442.09 per ounce by 12:08 p.m. ET (1608 GMT),\par having earlier jumped 5% to hit a peak since March 24 at $2,550.58. Platinum\par rose 0.1% to $976.06.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     IRON ORE\par     \par     Chinese steel futures prices fell on Monday, tracking lower raw material prices as\par still stringent COVID-19 related measures dented market sentiment and fuelled worries\par that the recovery in the world's second-largest economy might lose steam.\par     \par     The most-active construction material steel rebar on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shanghai Futures Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par , for October delivery, slipped 3.1% to 4,867 yuan ($763.76) a tonne at close.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     BASE METALS\par     \par      Aluminium prices fell to their lowest in more than three weeks on Monday as\par COVID-19 lockdowns in top consumer China fuelled worries about slowing demand for\par industrial metals.\par     \par     Benchmark aluminium on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Metal Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 LME\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) was down 4.1% at\par $3,235 a tonne at 1601 GMT. Prices of the metal used in transport, packaging and\par construction earlier touched $3,221, the lowest since March 17.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     OIL\par     \par      Oil prices fell about 4% on Monday, with Brent crude tumbling below $100 a barrel\par on worries that the pandemic will cut demand in China and as \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Energy\par Agency\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 IEA\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) countries plan to release record volumes of oil from strategic stocks.\par     \par     Brent futures fell $4.35, or 4.2%, to $98.43 a barrel by 1:46 p.m. EDT\par (1746 GMT), while U.S. West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude fell $3.75, or 3.8%,\par to $94.51.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     PALM OIL\par     \par     Malaysian palm oil futures closed at a near two-week high on Monday, extending last\par week's gains on small end-March inventories, although gains were limited by\par higher-than-expected production and a plunge in early-April exports.\par     \par     The benchmark palm oil contract for June delivery on the Bursa Malaysia\par Derivatives Exchange closed down 85 ringgit, or 1.44%, at 6,006 ringgit ($1,420.19) a\par tonne.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     RUBBER\par     \par     Japanese rubber futures treaded water on Monday, as support from a weaker yen and\par tight raw material supplies was countered by worries about Japan's economy.\par     \par     The Osaka Exchange rubber contract for September delivery,\par finished up 0.2 yen, or 0.1%, at 263.2 yen ($2.10) per kg.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -    \par    \par     \par \par  (Bengaluru Bureau; +91 80 6749 1130)\f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-4-11T21:41:39.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220411ei4b03i5p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
