\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei Rises 0.2%, Led by Tech Stocks -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2022\par 08:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0617 GMT - The Nikkei Stock Average closes 0.2% higher at 27787.98, led by gains in tech stocks, despite falls in banks and insurers. \uc2 Rakuten Group\uc2  gains 5.6% and medical-information platform operator M3 climbs 3.6%. Meanwhile, \uc2 Dai-ichi Life Holdings\uc2  loses 4.4% and \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group\uc2  drops 2.0% as the recent rising momentum in bond yields has eased. The 10-year Japanese government bond yield falls half a basis point to 0.205%. Broader market index Topix drops 0.2% at 1949.12. The war in Ukraine and its impact on trade remain in focus. USD/JPY is at 122.55, compared with 122.79 as of Monday 5 p.m. Eastern Time. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT - USD/SGD edges lower in thin trading amid a holiday in China, Hong Kong and Taiwan. On one hand, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair may be somewhat supported by reports of a push in Europe for new sanctions on Russia and signs of caution in U.S. equities, Maybank says. However, with markets also eyeing another possible MAS tightening in mid-April after Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightened in October 2021 and in late January 2022, Maybank retains its bias to lean against USD/SGD strength, it says. USD/SGD is down 0.1% at 1.3558. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0601 GMT - Austria's economic fundamentals are more solid than those of France, UniCredit Research says, expecting Austrian government bonds to outperform French OATs in the coming months. The Italian bank's rates strategists also expect the supply picture to favor the outperformance of Austrian government bonds versus German Bunds and French OATs. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  calculates that Austria has completed 38% of its annual bond issuance needs, compared with a eurozone average funding completion of 30%. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  also expects Austria's net government bond supply to be nil in the remainder of the year in light of EUR26 billion of redemptions. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0556 GMT - The upward trend in eurozone government bond yield comes to an end, according to UniCredit Research's expectations, and if bond markets stabilize at these or lower levels, the 10-year Austrian-German yield spread is likely to narrow further, the Italian bank's analysts say. They see room for the 10-year Austrian-German yield spread to stabilize in the 30-35 basis-point area. On Monday, the spread closed at 39 bps, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . Putting the yield spread moves in perspective, UniCredit Research says the 10-year Austrian-German bond yield spread reached 45 bps at the beginning of March, a period characterized by flight-to-quality. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0553 GMT - Austria's recently launched 0.90% February 2032-dated government bond looks appealing in relative-value terms and is likely to outperform longer-dated bonds if yields stabilize, UniCredit Research says. The bond is on offer at Austria's auction on Tuesday along with the 1.50% February 2047-dated one. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  likes the February 2047-dated bond versus the 0.75% March 2051-dated peer, but the 30-year segment of the Austrian yield curve is expensive, it says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT - This morning's reported comments from \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Gov. \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  hint at some unease with the yen's swift and sharp weakness of late, SPI Asset Management says. Japanese companies tend not to welcome increased \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility due to higher hedging costs, and Kuroda has this audience in mind, SPI Asset Management notes. Despite verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently, any \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to have a lasting effect and usually only offers better levels to sell yen unless accompanied by a policy twist, adds SPI Asset Management, which expects a weaker yen. USD/JPY is 0.2% lower at 122.55. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0332 GMT - Malaysia's current account balance is set to get a boost this year due to higher commodity prices, especially for palm oil and petroleum amid Russia-Ukraine crisis, says \uc2 Fitch Solutions\uc2  . It says these two categories of products make up over 16% of Malaysia's total exports. Services exports are also likely to improve due to the reopening of travel with other economies including Singapore, says the researcher. It estimates the current account surplus for the Southeast Asia nation to come in at 1.9% of GDP this year, wider than the 1.6% recorded in 2021. (chester.tay@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT - Higher-than-expected commodity prices are prompting \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  to project that inflation in South Korea will far exceed its February forecast of 3.1% for 2022. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has yet to give a new forecast but says it expects consumer inflation to remain above 4% for the time being as prices of crude oil and grains surge in the wake of the Russia-Ukraine war. The \uc2 BOK\uc2  is expected to update its 2022 inflation forecast in May. Its benchmark consumer-price index rose a faster-than-expected 4.1% from a year earlier in March, marking the highest level since December 2011 and remaining above its 2% target for 12 consecutive months. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0234 GMT - JPY strengthens against G-10 and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid mild losses in U.S. stock futures and concerns over the Covid-19-related lockdown in Shanghai, China. There are few signs of virus containment in the city of more than 25 million people as local cases keep rising rapidly, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. That dims hopes of a full reopening this week and China's lockdowns are likely to lead to a significant negative impact on the economy, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  adds. USD/JPY falls 0.2% to 122.50, AUD/JPY drops 0.3% to 92.38 and EUR/JPY is down 0.2% at 134.40. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT - Asian oil-and-gas importers face an uncertain outlook on the continuation of Russian supply after it asked for payments for energy-commodity deliveries in rubles, \uc2 Fitch Solutions\uc2  says. Six markets in Russia's crosshairs that have sizable energy imports from the country: Australia, Japan, New Zealand, Singapore, South Korea and Taiwan. For instance, South Korea gets 6.4% of its crude and 20% of its fuel imports from Russia, while 15% of Taiwan's liquefied natural gas is from Russia, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  says. "Regional trading hub Singapore is also home to Russian distillates and fuel oil." Any disruption to commodity trade "poses upside risk to already elevated energy prices in Asia," \uc2 Fitch\uc2  adds. (yongchang.chin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT - Inflation and expected interest-rate increases are a headwind to Australian consumer confidence and consumption, but Macquarie says that will be offset by other factors. The investment bank says households' current balance-sheet strength will mitigate some of the risk, while the additional A$8.6 billion earmarked for consumers in the recent federal budget--including one-off payments to some Australians and a cut in fuel taxes--will lift near-term spending. Macquarie notes that February sales maintained upward momentum with 1.8% growth on month, as cafes, restaurants and catering performed strongly, according to latest government figures. (mike.cherney@wsj.com; @Mike_Cherney)\par \par \uc2 0018 GMT - Japanese stocks are modestly higher, led by gains in electronics stocks, as the yen's recent weakening supports the market despite uncertainty over the war in Ukraine. \uc2 Panasonic Corp\uc2  . advances 2.3% and \uc2 Renesas Electronics\uc2  is 2.1% higher. Among individual movers, Shimamura gains 6.2% after the apparel retailer projected a 5.0% rise in FY net profit. USD/JPY is at 122.80, compared with 122.67 as of Monday's Tokyo stock market close. Investors remain focused on commodity prices and global trade implications as the war in Ukraine continues. The Nikkei Stock Average is up 0.4% at 27854.18. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 05, 2022 02:17 ET (06:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220405ei45000cv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
