\par\par \b \uc2 Yen May Still Weaken Despite \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Reportedly Signaling Concern -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2022\par 05:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0350 GMT - This morning's reported comments from \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Gov. \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  hint at some unease with the yen's swift and sharp weakness of late, SPI Asset Management says. Japanese companies tend not to welcome increased \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility due to higher hedging costs, and Kuroda has this audience in mind, SPI Asset Management notes. Despite verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently, any \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to have a lasting effect and usually only offers better levels to sell yen unless accompanied by a policy twist, adds SPI Asset Management, which expects a weaker yen. USD/JPY is 0.2% lower at 122.55. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0332 GMT - Malaysia's current account balance is set to get a boost this year due to higher commodity prices, especially for palm oil and petroleum amid Russia-Ukraine crisis, says \uc2 Fitch Solutions\uc2  . It says these two categories of products make up over 16% of Malaysia's total exports. Services exports are also likely to improve due to the reopening of travel with other economies including Singapore, says the researcher. It estimates the current account surplus for the Southeast Asia nation to come in at 1.9% of GDP this year, wider than the 1.6% recorded in 2021. (chester.tay@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT - Higher-than-expected commodity prices are prompting \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  to project that inflation in South Korea will far exceed its February forecast of 3.1% for 2022. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has yet to give a new forecast but says it expects consumer inflation to remain above 4% for the time being as prices of crude oil and grains surge in the wake of the Russia-Ukraine war. The \uc2 BOK\uc2  is expected to update its 2022 inflation forecast in May. Its benchmark consumer-price index rose a faster-than-expected 4.1% from a year earlier in March, marking the highest level since December 2011 and remaining above its 2% target for 12 consecutive months. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0234 GMT - JPY strengthens against G-10 and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid mild losses in U.S. stock futures and concerns over the Covid-19-related lockdown in Shanghai, China. There are few signs of virus containment in the city of more than 25 million people as local cases keep rising rapidly, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. That dims hopes of a full reopening this week and China's lockdowns are likely to lead to a significant negative impact on the economy, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  adds. USD/JPY falls 0.2% to 122.50, AUD/JPY drops 0.3% to 92.38 and EUR/JPY is down 0.2% at 134.40. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT - Asian oil-and-gas importers face an uncertain outlook on the continuation of Russian supply after it asked for payments for energy-commodity deliveries in rubles, \uc2 Fitch Solutions\uc2  says. Six markets in Russia's crosshairs that have sizable energy imports from the country: Australia, Japan, New Zealand, Singapore, South Korea and Taiwan. For instance, South Korea gets 6.4% of its crude and 20% of its fuel imports from Russia, while 15% of Taiwan's liquefied natural gas is from Russia, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  says. "Regional trading hub Singapore is also home to Russian distillates and fuel oil." Any disruption to commodity trade "poses upside risk to already elevated energy prices in Asia," \uc2 Fitch\uc2  adds. (yongchang.chin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT - Inflation and expected interest-rate increases are a headwind to Australian consumer confidence and consumption, but Macquarie says that will be offset by other factors. The investment bank says households' current balance-sheet strength will mitigate some of the risk, while the additional A$8.6 billion earmarked for consumers in the recent federal budget--including one-off payments to some Australians and a cut in fuel taxes--will lift near-term spending. Macquarie notes that February sales maintained upward momentum with 1.8% growth on month, as cafes, restaurants and catering performed strongly, according to latest government figures. (mike.cherney@wsj.com; @Mike_Cherney)\par \par \uc2 0018 GMT - Japanese stocks are modestly higher, led by gains in electronics stocks, as the yen's recent weakening supports the market despite uncertainty over the war in Ukraine. \uc2 Panasonic Corp\uc2  . advances 2.3% and \uc2 Renesas Electronics\uc2  is 2.1% higher. Among individual movers, Shimamura gains 6.2% after the apparel retailer projected a 5.0% rise in FY net profit. USD/JPY is at 122.80, compared with 122.67 as of Monday's Tokyo stock market close. Investors remain focused on commodity prices and global trade implications as the war in Ukraine continues. The Nikkei Stock Average is up 0.4% at 27854.18. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0008 GMT - As stimulus from Australia's fiscal 2023 budget a week ago begins to reach the already-strong economy, some economists are warning it was a case of overkill. From a purely macro economic perspective, the government didn't need to spend more on the cost of living allowances, says \uc2 Shane Oliver\uc2  , economist at AMP Capital. Enough money has already been pumped into households over the last two years. This is reflected in the roughly A$250 billion in excess household savings that has built up as a lot of the stimulus was simply saved, he adds. "The economy is already very strong. And it just makes the \uc2 RBA\uc2  's job in controlling inflation harder," he adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2056 GMT - The benchmark S&P/TSX Composite Index gains 0.6% to 22085 and the blue-chip S&P/TSX 60 Index climbs 0.7% to 1336. One market mover today: Orvana Minerals, up 7.2%. That's after it restarted Spanish \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the suspension of a three-week national transportation strike there. Meanwhile, a record number of Canadian firms expect significant increases in the prices they pay for goods and what they charge customers as a result of labor shortages, supply-chain constraints and robust consumer demand, according to the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  's latest business-outlook survey. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2048 GMT - The IPC stock index closes down 0.5% from Friday's record finish at 56313 points, with \uc2 America Movil\uc2  shares down 0.6% and retailer shares off 3.1%. Arca Continental shares close down 0.4%. The Coke bottler says it plans to invest MXN12B in 2022, half in Mexico and the rest in the US and Latin America. The peso extends its rally, trading at 19.82 to the US dollar from 19.86 Friday. Statistics institute Inegi reports a 0.4-point rise in March consumer confidence to 43.9, with respondents more optimistic about their household situation and prospects than about the overall economy. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2030 GMT - The \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  says ASL Capital Markets became its first truly new primary dealer since May 2019. Primary dealers make markets in Treasury securities, underwrite government debt auctions and serve as counterparties to Fed open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These large financial firms were once the Fed's main point of contact with markets, but new ways of conducting monetary policy have significantly broadened who the Fed does business with, and made the roster of primary dealers less important. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1946 GMT - As the Fed tightens, investors are again exploring the effect of interest-rate differentials on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  's Ivan Asensio tells WSJ. "The carry trade is back," he says, noting that the Fed is expected to raise rates faster than other developed economies, so it makes sense to borrow at their lower rates to buy dollar assets paying a higher yield. "Some of the bullishness in the dollar [versus] some of the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the euro, the yen is [due to] large institutional flows coming to the US because all of a sudden the dollar looks like an attractive carry trade," Asensio says. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 04, 2022 23:50 ET (03:50 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220405ei450004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
