\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  unlikely to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2 , hike rates to strengthen yen -former  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  tsar Shinohara\b0\par\par\uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 30 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will find it hard to directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market or raise interest rates to combat recent falls in the yen, which are likely to persist as its economic strength weakens, former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat \uc2 Naoyuki Shinohara\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Shinohara, who oversaw Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy during the Lehman crisis in 2008, said Japan may face prolonged periods of yen weakness because of its huge fiscal deficit, worsening external position and ultra-low interest rates.\par \par \uc2 But yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or raising the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ultra-low interest rates is unlikely to have a lasting effect in reversing the trend of a weak yen, added Shinohara, who retains close ties to global and domestic policymakers.\par \par \uc2 "It's meaningless to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which could have a short-term effect on the yen, but can't be carried out indefinitely," Shinohara told Reuters on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Raising the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's yield targets will not push up the yen by much either, as any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move will be far more modest than steady rate hikes planned by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 , he added.\par \par \uc2 "Japanese authorities don't need to panic now, as the yen doesn't seem to spiraling lower," Shinohara said. "In fact, the current weak yen reflects economic fundamentals to some extent."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's defence of its yield cap with offers to buy unlimited amounts of 10-year government bonds showed that it did not view recent declines in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a problem, he said.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's position appears to be that a weak yen is good for the economy," Shinohara said. "It probably doesn't see the yen's current levels as dangerous."\par \par \uc2 Japanese policymakers have recently stepped up warnings against sharp yen falls.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the incumbent top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat, Masato Kanda, said Tokyo and Washington agreed to communicate closely on exchange rate issues, a remark that fuelled market speculation about the chances of a yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It's unskilful to look solely at yen levels in deciding when to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Shinohara responded, when asked at what level Tokyo could seriously consider stepping into the market.\par \par \uc2 "The speed of yen moves also must be taken into account," he added. "I don't think the pace of yen falls now is very fast."\par \par \uc2 Following his role as Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs until 2009, Shinohara was deputy managing director of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  until 2015.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Editing by Clarence Fernandez) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-3-30T07:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220330ei3u00q32\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
