\par\par \b \uc2 US Credit Spreads Look 'Too Tight' -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 10:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0808 GMT - Expectations that the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  will raise interest rates by 50 basis points in both May and June meetings suggests credit spreads trade too tight, says \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  . "A quick U.S. tightening like this over the next quarter leaves credit spreads still too tight, and equities slightly stretched," analysts at the Japanese bank say. They expect the combination of lower economic growth and elevated inflation coupled with tighter monetary policy to weigh on companies. "The stagflation theme will erode corporate earnings, and the tighter monetary policy will increase the cost of funding for corporates," they say. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0742 GMT - The drop in the \uc2 GfK\uc2  consumer sentiment headline to a 22-month low is a clear sign that the war in Ukraine and higher inflation are weighing heavy on consumer sentiment, Pantheon Macroeconomics says. The details of the indicator show that the surge in energy prices and overall inflation is putting a strain on general consumer sentiment, Pantheon says. The willingness-to-buy index edged down, reaching its lowest level since April 2020, which Pantheon says is unsurprising given the drop in income expectations by 25 points, falling to its lowest level since January 2009. "Consumers are seeing their purchasing power melt away," Pantheon says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0740 GMT - The dollar looks undervalued in the near-term after failing to rise materially on the recent sharp rise in short-term \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, ING says. The dollar has only received moderate support from the jump in short-term U.S. yields, driven by market bets for multiple 50 basis points interest rate rises by the Federal Reserve, ING analysts say. "Looking at short-term valuation, the dollar is looking cheap against most G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the recent moves in yields," they say. However, the dollar should ultimately catch up with the move in yields, they say. The DXY dollar index falls 0.2% to 99.9410 following gains on Monday. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0738 GMT - The U.S. economy remained largely healthy through early March, but the war in Ukraine is worsening pricing pressures tied to continued supply-chain disruptions, S&P Global Ratings says. The credit rating agency expects the U.S. economy to grow 3.2% this year, down from the 3.9% forecast in November. "The main drivers for weaker than expected growth this year and the next are continued supply chain disruptions, exacerbated by the Russia-Ukraine conflict; higher prices, particularly for food and energy; and much more aggressive Fed policy to fight these higher prices," S&P Global Ratings says. The firm expects seven rate increases this year, and a 50-basis point raise in May. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0736 GMT - The euro will struggle to climb sustainably above $1.10 against the dollar without positive news on negotiations between Ukraine and Russia, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. The Ukraine war has increased pressure on prices, fueling expectations that the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may look to tighten monetary policy more quickly. However, as long as the war in Ukraine continues and the risk of further sanctions remains, the \uc2 ECB\uc2  is likely to continue to seem "hesitant" compared with another central banks, which won't help the euro, it says. EUR/USD is last up 0.3% at 1.1014. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0734 GMT - The war in Ukraine made a noticeable dent in French consumer sentiment toward the end of the first quarter, Pantheon says. The consumer confidence index in France dropped to 91 in March from a revised reading of 97 in February. Households in France are, like their counterparts in Germany, increasingly concerned that sustained high inflation will depress living standards and savings over the next 12 months, Pantheon says. The headline was pulled lower by a sharp fall in consumers' outlook for their financial situation in the next 12 months, a fall in households' expected savings capacity and a leap in inflation expectations, Pantheon notes. Purchasing intentions also weakened, albeit only slightly, and households' fear of unemployment increased too, Pantheon says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0719 GMT - The U.K.'s GDP likely increased 1% in 1Q on-quarter despite the hit from the Covid-19 Omicron variant at the beginning of the year, but the cost-of-living crisis will weigh on the economic recovery in 2Q, Pantheon Macroeconomics says. Consumer spending will be hit by a 2% fall in real household incomes due to the rise in utility bills and the higher tax burden, the economic-research firm says. "We think that GDP will fall by 0.3% in 2Q, reversing one third of 1Q's increase, and then rise slowly in the second half of the year," Pantheon says. "We think that the Monetary Policy Committee will refrain from hiking bank rate further in 2H, after increasing it to 1.00% in May." (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0717 GMT - German import prices rose 26.3% on year in February, mainly due to higher energy prices, the German statistics office Destatis says. Energy imports were 129.5% more expensive than in February 2021, Destatis says. This increase was driven by a 256.5% rise in the price of natural gas and a 70.3% increase in the price of crude oil on year. Import prices, excluding crude oil and mineral-oil products, increased by 23.3%, Destatis says. Export prices rose 12.4% in February compared with the previous year. Compared with the previous month, German import prices rose 1.3% in February and export prices increased 1.0%, Destatis says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0704 GMT - The possibility of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. to stop the yen's depreciation seems unlikely, says Daisaku Ueno, a strategist at \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities\uc2  . That's because it is more favorable for the U.S. to have a strong dollar, which helps ease inflationary pressures. It is also difficult for the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to raise interest rates just to stop the yen's fall because its rate increases would need to match the pace of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  's in order to influence the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the Japanese economy isn't strong enough to bear such a radical move. (megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0703 GMT - The Russian ruble rises to a four-week high versus the dollar on hopes for a breakthrough in peace talks between Russia and Ukraine. USD/RUB falls to as low as 92.4999. Another round of Russia-Ukraine talks are set to get underway on Tuesday in Turkey and Ukraine President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2  has said his country could declare neutrality to end the conflict. The ruble is supported by optimism surrounding the talks as well as mandatory sales of Russian exporters' foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues, Unicredit Research says. "At the same time, however, investors remain prudent about new effective steps towards a diplomatic solution to the conflict." (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0701 GMT - Reaching 0.70% is within the realm of possible outcomes for the 10-year German Bund yield, mostly dragged by U.S. markets, but it isn't a done deal, say ING's rates strategists. "The main risk to our view is a deterioration in sentiment," they add. They point to geopolitics, but add that more broadly, sharp rises in rates have had a tendency to generate their own risk-off reactions. The 10-year Bund yield is trading 3 basis points higher at 0.610%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0648 GMT - South Korea's Kospi added 0.4% to close at 2741.07, supported by gains in auto, airline and tech stocks. Renewed hopes for fresh ceasefire talks between Russia and Ukraine helped buoy investor sentiment, stoking risk appetite. USD/KRW settled 0.6% lower at 1,219.80 in Seoul trade on risk-on sentiment. Auto-components maker \uc2 Mando\uc2  jumped 8.6% on hopes that its 1Q earnings could beat street views. Car maker \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  gained 1.2%. Budget airline \uc2 Jin Air\uc2  rose 4.7%. Memory-chip maker \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2  added 1.7%. Meanwhile, \uc2 Korea Electric Power\uc2  lost 2.8% after a government decision against an additional tariffs increase in 2Q. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  was 0.7% higher. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 29, 2022 04:08 ET (08:08 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220329ei3t000vx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
