\par\par \b \uc2 Japanese Yen Seen Remaining Vulnerable to Rising Yields -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 13:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1116 GMT - Indications that Japan will take action to curb yen volatility could offer the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  some support but that will play second fiddle to moves in Japanese government bond yields, ING says. "The yen remains highly vulnerable as long as bond yields continue to press higher--and a return to [USD/JPY] 125 today is not off the charts," ING analysts say. Japan's 10-year yield remains dangerously close to the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  's 0.25% target despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bond market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they say. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat, Masato Kanda, on Tuesday said Japan and the U.S. agreed communicate closely on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues. USD/JPY falls 0.2% to 123.537 after hitting its highest level since August 2015 at 125.036 on Monday. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1037 GMT - The cost of insuring against European junk bond defaults fall sharply Tuesday as investors remain optimistic amid Ukraine-Russia peace talks in Turkey. The iTraxx Europe Crossover index, which tracks euro high-yield credit default swaps, falls 15 basis points to a seven-day low of 346 bps in early trading from Monday's close, \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  data shows." A sense of positivity returned to financial markets as the prospects of more ceasefire talks between Russia and Ukraine soothed investor jitters," FXTM says. Stock markets continue to rise, with the pan-continental Stoxx Europe 600 jumping 1.3% and S&P 500 futures up 0.3%. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1009 GMT - With eurozone inflation set to average around 6% this year, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  expects aggregate real household incomes to fall by about 1.5%. The U.K. economic-research firm has reduced its eurozone household spending forecast for this year from 5.5% to 4.0%. "The impact on consumption will be cushioned to some extent by fiscal support and, to a greater extent, by a decline in the saving rate," \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. This should help the economy avoid a recession but \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  still thinks that GDP growth will be weaker than either consensus or \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expectations this year, forecasting growth of only 2.8%. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1006 GMT - \uc2 Barclays\uc2  faces reputation issues following a bond blunder, says \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2  . The British bank said it issued more securities than it had registered for sale in the U.S., meaning it will have to repurchase the affected bonds at their original price, taking a loss of GBP450 million. "This is a highly embarrassing turn of events for \uc2 Barclays\uc2  ," HL says, adding that stock investors are clearly irked about the mismanagement of the structured product sales and the quick fire purchase, which sees the bank nursing a heavy loss. The blunder has also delayed the bumper GBP1 billion share buy back, adding toshareholders' angst, HL says. Still, underlying performance of the business is positive, and the bank is well capitalised, with a CET1 radio higher than the regulatory minimum, it adds. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  ' shares fall 3.3%. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0935 GMT - The U.K. yield curve has flattened, suggesting investors are concerned about economic growth, says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  . "The fact that the gilt curve flattened despite this pricing out of rate hikes is noteworthy, as it points to the market focusing upon the scale of the headwinds facing the U.K. economy rather than the modest reduction in rate hike expectations," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  analysts say. A more cautious tone from the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  at its March 17 meeting, due to a looming squeeze on real household incomes, led investors to unwind bets that interest rates could rise more aggressively, even by 50 basis points, in coming meetings. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0935 GMT - Government bond issuance in the eurozone is expected to drop slightly in 2Q compared with 1Q but the economic impact of the Ukraine war represents uncertainty for national budgets, Elmar Voelker, senior fixed income analyst at LBBW, says in a note. "The possible negative impact of the war in Ukraine on the budget situation of the countries is a major unknown," he says. LBBW expects gross government bond issuance of eurozone countries to ease by 3% to around EUR325 billion in 2Q from 1Q. LBBW is of the view that crisis-related additional financing requirements will initially be covered primarily by higher money market issuance, but Voelker says there is a considerable upside risk for capital market issuance as well. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT - U.K. borrowing costs have been falling across all maturities since the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  relaxed its stance on the outlook of interest-rate rises at its March meeting, says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  . On Monday, "gilt yields finished lower across the curve, with the 2-year down by 5.4bp, the 10-year by 7.8bp and the 30-year by 10.5bp," analysts at the Dutch bank say. The move was triggered by "a dovish turn from the Bank of England Governor Bailey who noted that the Bank had softened its guidance at its 17th March meeting owing to a looming squeeze on real household incomes that is set to exceed that seen in the 1970s." U.K. 10-year gilt yields last trade at 1.69% and two-year yields at 1.39%, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0923 GMT - S&P Global Ratings has wasted no time in rushing to affirm Australia's AAA sovereign credit rating and stable outlook following the release of the Australian federal government's budget for 2022-2023 at 0830 GMT. Australia's economic recovery is improving and the government's fiscal trajectory faster than it previously anticipated, S&P says. Federal and state governments' budget deficits will narrow toward 3% of GDP over the next two years. The budget revealed a materially lower deficit in fiscal 2022 of 3.9% of GDP compared with a forecast of 5.5% of GDP made a year ago. Australia remains in a small group of 11 sovereigns with a AAA rating from S&P. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0913 GMT - Sterling could fall below the key 1.30 level versus the dollar in the near-term as the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is expected to raise interest rates by less than the Federal Reserve, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. "While Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  and other Fed officials have been talking up the potential need for larger 50 basis points rate hikes at upcoming FOMC meetings, the BOE is continuing to signal more caution over the need for further monetary tightening in the near-term," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  analysts say. This divergence in rhetoric has been reflected in market pricing with the BOE expected to deliver a cumulative total of 136bp rate rises by year-end, compared to 212bp for the Fed, they say. GBP/USD falls 0.3% to a 13-day low of 1.3051, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0901 GMT - The Australian government's net debt will fall slowly over the next decade, according to the projections in the latest federal budget. Net debt is tipped to drop to 26.9% by 2032-2033 from 31.1% of GDP in 2022-2023. Australia's debt burden isn't as great as those of other major economies, but still leaves the economy more vulnerable to external shocks. Gross debt is expected to peak at 44.9% of GDP in mid-2025 before falling to 40.3% of GDP over the same period. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0857 GMT - Despite two years of battling the Covid-19 pandemic, Australia's budget forecasts the economy will grow by 3.5% in 2022-23 and approach full employment in the next year, with the jobless rate set to fall to its lowest levels since the early 1970s. Inflation is forecast to rise somewhat, but not blow out, avoiding the biggest threat to the economic outlook. The government also expects wages growth of around 3.25% in 2022-23, something that will give the \uc2 RBA\uc2  a green light to start normalizing interest rates. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0852 GMT - Australia's 2022-2023 federal budget is focused on cushioning the blow of rising energy and food costs, pledging cuts to fuel taxes, tax relief to low- and middle-income earners, and delivering a one-off payment to nearly one quarter of Australians to support household budgets. With the Liberal-National coalition government trailing badly in opinion polls ahead of an election due by May, the cash splash is aimed at finding traction with the electorate and reversing a fall in consumer confidence. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 29, 2022 07:16 ET (11:16 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220329ei3t001g4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
