\par\par \b \uc2 Japanese Yen Seen Weakening Further After \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Bond-Market  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 13:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1101 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  's decision to make two offers to buy an unlimited amount of government bonds failed to send yields lower and added pressure on the Japanese yen, ING says. The yen has scope to fall further versus the dollar given the bond market weakness on growing \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  policy tightening expectations and rising energy prices, which are negative for the Japanese economy, ING analysts say. A rise in USD/JPY to 130 is "well within reach in the near term unless the bond environment improves," they say. USD/JPY rises 1.8% to 124.272 after earlier hitting 125.036, its highest level since August 2015, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1019 GMT - The dollar may rise further if the March U.S. nonfarm payrolls report on Friday bolsters the market's interest rate rise expectations for the Federal Reserve, Monex Europe says. "Should wage growth continue to tick up despite the recent increase in labour supply, money markets are likely to fully price two 50 basis points rate hikes from the Fed in May and June," Monex says. In that case the dollar will rally broadly across the G10 space, it says. The DXY dollar index rises 0.4% to 99.1610 after earlier hitting a near three-week high of 99.3080. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1015 GMT - The euro is likely to rise versus the pound but less than previously thought due to the Ukraine war, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  says as it cuts its year-end EUR/GBP forecast to 0.85 from 0.89 previously. The market continues to attach a high risk premium to holding the euro as it believes the European economy will be hardest hit by the Russia-Ukraine conflict, but \uc2 BofA\uc2  analysts are skeptical when it comes to the pound. "Ultimately...we think the U.K. [fiscal and monetary] policy mix, deteriorating fundamentals and increased pressures in current account financing will all be factors that weigh on GBP." EUR/GBP last trades at 0.8355. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0935 GMT - The Turkish lira could extend its losses as \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to raise interest rates even if inflation surges further on soaring energy prices caused by the Ukraine war, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. "Interest rates and monetary policy are unlikely to change any time soon, but inflation will continue to rise and with it the economic and in the end also the fiscal difficulties," \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Antje Praefcke\uc2  says in a note. That means there's still "enormous underlying depreciation" potential for the lira, she says. The only question is when and why this potential is realized, she says. USD/TRY rises 0.1% to 14.8485. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0913 GMT - Risk premiums and issuance volumes show sings of recovery in European corporate bond markets after the eruption of war in Ukraine upended markets, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  's research team. "The latest credit market performance confirms a normalization in credit risk premiums," analysts at the Italian bank say. They note that both iBoxx senior financial and nonfinancial corporate bond indexes are both trading seven basis points below their levels before the Russia-Ukraine crisis broke out, while hybrids and nonfinancials are 33bp and 25bp below that level respectively. Issuance of euro investment-grade nonfinancial corporate bonds reached EUR29 billion so far this year, the strongest volume since March last year, while a high-yield nonfinancial firm issued a bond for the first time since mid-February last week, they say. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0858 GMT - Heineken's announcement of its intention to sell its business in Russia is unlikely to move the Dutch brewer's corporate bond spreads, says ING. "Following the strategic review Heineken concluded it is no longer sustainable nor viable to continue ownership of the Russian business," says ING, adding that it likes Heineken fundamentally and does not expect this news to have an impact on its credit curve. Heineken expects and impairment of EUR400 million and will guarantee the payment of salaries for their 1,800 employees in Russia until the end of 2022. Russia represents 2% of Heineken's revenue, it says. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0831 GMT - USD/JPY rises sharply following the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  's announcement of additional fixed-rate bond-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, which raises the prospects of a widening U.S.-Japan yield gap. Besides the U.S.-Japan policy divergence, Japan's growing trade deficit--due partly to higher crude-oil prices--is also pushing the yen weaker, says Akira Moroga, chief market strategist at \uc2 Aozora Bank\uc2  . Nonetheless, Japanese financial officials appear focused on limiting bond-yield gains, Moroga says, adding that "there is little sense of urgency about the yen's weakening" because Japanese stocks have been relatively stable. USD/JPY rises to 124.11, its highest level since August 2015, up from 122.03 late Friday in New York. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0824 GMT - Signs of decent investor appetite for euro corporate bonds last week suggests demand may remain satisfactory, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  's research team. "Last week's performance...suggests decent appetite for European credit risk in general, implying limited downside risks in the asset class [this week]," analysts at the Italian bank say. Last week, spreads on the iBoxx investment grade nonfinancial senior index tightened by six basis points and those on the iBoxx financial senior index fell by nine basis points, they say. Moreover, the iBoxx high yield nonfinancial index has tightened by 18 basis points during the same period, they say. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0814 GMT - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to delay its monetary policy normalization somewhat relative to current guidance as deteriorating confidence points to a shock in demand, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says. However, policy tightening pressures are likely to reemerge quickly once fiscal support is implemented and inflation accelerates further due to renewed damage on supply chains, the U.S. bank says. "Looking ahead, if these persistent supply dynamics dominate, the \uc2 ECB\uc2  may need to raise rates faster," it says. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  expects headline inflation in the eurozone to average 7.8% in 2022, up by 2.8 percentage points since the beginning of the war in Ukraine. Inflation is expected to fall back to around 2% in 2023, \uc2 GS\uc2  says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0753 GMT - European corporate bond markets are starting the week on shaky ground, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  's research team. A softer tone in \uc2 German government\uc2  bonds and mixed trading in stocks signal euro credit looks uncertain to do well this week, analysts at the Italian bank say. The absence of key data releases until Friday indicates that euro corporate bond markets will be driven by news headlines, "with inflation expectations and the Russia-Ukraine crisis sitting in the driver's seat," they say. The pan-continental Stoxx Europe 600 advances 0.7%, while futures tied to the S&P 500 fall 0.2%.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT - The pound weakens against a broadly firmer dollar, which rises on prospects of frequent interest-rate rises by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  . In the absence of major U.K. economic data this week the pound will likely be driven by external factors and "may well extend its current downtrend" toward $1.30, ING analysts say. The U.K. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more likely to rise against the euro, however. "We continue to see the euro as more exposed to any dollar strength given the higher sensitivity to energy prices and greater monetary policy divergence with the U.S.," they say, though note that EUR/GBP is unlikely to weaken much below 0.8300 for now. GBP/USD falls 0.2% to 1.3159, while EUR/GBP falls 0.1% to 0.8332. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0743 GMT - The eurozone economy is expected to expand 3% this year, 0.2 percentage points less than previously anticipated, as the war in Ukraine and higher, longer-than-expected inflation dents consumer spending, Pantheon Macroeconomics says. Recent sentiment data in Germany points to downside risks for 2Q, while the outlook in Spain for 2H is also clouded by a hit to real incomes due to high energy prices, the economic-research firm says. "When we say that the eurozone economy will grow by 3% in 2022, 2.4 percentage points of that is due to a positive carry over from 2021," Pantheon says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 28, 2022 07:01 ET (11:01 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220328ei3s001ai\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
