\par\par \b \uc2 Stocks look past rate risks in late rally, yen wilts\b0\par\par\uc2 850 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 23:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Reuters) -World stock markets cast aside fears of rising interest rates on Monday with the tech-heavy U.S. Nasdaq index rallying 1.3%, even as parts of the Treasury yield curve signaled recession risks while oil prices tumbled on fears of weaker Chinese demand.\par \par \uc2 After a choppy session where stocks oscillated between gains and losses, U.S. shares finally broke higher, with electric car marker \uc2 Tesla\uc2  surging almost 8% on news that it will seek investor approval for a stock split.\par \par \uc2 But the buoyancy in stocks was foreshadowed by several signs and analyst warnings that it may not last, as global interest rates will likely climb higher this year and could drag on economic growth.\par \par \uc2 In the Treasuries market, for example, the yield curve for two- and 10-year Treasuries presaged growing recession risks on Monday, even though the curve for three-month bills and 10-year Treasuries still pointed to robust economic expansion.\par \par \uc2 Expectations of rising global interest rates prolonged a sell-off in European government debt, enabling Dutch and Belgian two-year bond yields to turn positive for the first time since 2014. [GVD/EUR]\par \par \uc2 The tide of rising global yields led Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to declare on Monday a steadfast attempt to defend its 0.25% yield cap, vowing to buy an unlimited amount of government bonds for the first four days of the week.\par \par \uc2 The announcement sent the yen reeling to a six-year low at one point. Unlike other major economies that are battling surging price pressures, inflation in Japan remains well below its 2% target.\par \par \uc2 By early evening, MSCI's gauge of stocks across the globe gained 0.39%.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite jumped 1.31%, the Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 0.27% and the S&P 500 climbed 0.71%.\par \par \uc2 A lockdown in China's financial hub of Shanghai to contain surging COVID-19 cases, on the other hand, weighed on Chinese shares and dragged on oil prices, as investors anticipated weaker demand from the world's No 2 economy.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude fell a whopping 9.14% to $103.49 per barrel and Brent skidded 9.1% to $109.70. [O/R]\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Japanese yen shed 1.4% versus the dollar to 123.87 per dollar, after skidding as much as 2.5% at one point to notch its biggest one-day drop since March 2020.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market or raise rates to defend the yen if it weakens beyond 130 to the dollar, the country's former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat, Eisuke Sakakibara, said.\par \par \uc2 TECH STOCK RALLY COULD STALL\par \par \uc2 With some investors betting that U.S. rates could rise by 50 basis points in April, analysts warned that stocks could succumb to deeper losses in coming months.\par \par \uc2 "One of the more confounding developments during the past two weeks has been the strength of the rebound in the tech-heavy Nasdaq-100 Index at the same time interest rates soared to cycle highs," said \uc2 Lisa Shalett\uc2 , head of the global investment office at \uc2 Morgan Stanley Wealth Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "As this and other yield curves head toward inversion, the nascent rebound in megacap tech stocks may stall."\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yield curve, as measured by the gap between five and 30-year yields, inverted on Monday for the first time since early 2006, as a sell-off in the bond market resumed, with short-dated yields jumping to their highest since 2019.\par \par \uc2 Fixed-income analysts said the inverted yield curve indicated that some investors believe the Fed's policy tightening will put the brakes on economic growth. [US/]\par \par \uc2 The two-year Treasury yield climbed to 2.334%, from 2.299%, while 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields retreated to 2.459%, after initially pushing above the 2.5%-marker for the first time since 2019 [US/].\par \par \uc2 Francois Savary, chief investment officer at Swiss wealth management firm Prime Partners, said portfolio rebalancing ahead of quarter-end helped explained strength in equities in the face of surging bond yields.\par \par \uc2 "A day of reckoning is coming because at the start of April you have earnings season and you will get a sense of the impact of rising energy prices and guidance for the future," he said\par \par \uc2 "I would not bet on the rally continuing in a straight line," Savary added.\par \par \uc2 Euro zone bonds continued their move into positive-yield territory, while money market pricing suggested investors were now anticipating 60 bps worth of rate hikes from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  by year-end compared with 50 bps last week.\par \par \uc2 British 10-year bond yields hit their highest levels in six years, Swiss 10-year yields and Australian three-year bond yields rose to their highest levels since 2014.\par \par \uc2 In commodity markets, gold softened to $1,931 an ounce, down about 1.35%. [GOL/]\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Dhara Ranasinghe Additional reporting by Sujata Rao in London and Wayne Cole in SyndeyEditing by Tomasz Janowski, Mark Potter and Cynthia Osterman) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-3-28T21:13:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220328ei3s001md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
