Connect in Brussels, Belgium \u8226\'20\'22 23:50 \uc2 BOJ\uc2  releases summary of opinions from board members at its Mar. 17-18 policy meeting Commentary and Analysis EUR/USD traders continue to buy and see pair fall \u8226\'20\'22 Between Mar 15-22 EUR/USD traders added to an existing long position \u8226\'20\'22 Pair traded 1.0921-1.1137 before dipping to 1.0961 \u8226\'20\'22 Today's range 1.0945-1.0986 EBS. Few of any remaining longs are in the money \u8226\'20\'22 Scope for a decline towards base 20-day Bollinger bands at 1.0845 \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. retail sales data today. EUR 580 million expiries at 1.1000 \u8226\'20\'22 Mar 14/15/16 lows: 1.0900, 1.0921, 1.0948, 61.8% 1.0806-1.1137 rise 1.0933 USD/JPY jumps to new multi-year highs, \uc2 BOJ\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in bonds \u8226\'20\'22 Yen tumbles as \uc2 BOJ\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep bond yields pinned down \u8226\'20\'22 On Friday Kuroda extolled the benefits of a weaker yen \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY rose to a new multi-year high above 124.00 option barriers on Monday \u8226\'20\'22 Spot looks set to soar through the 2015 125.86 peak soon \u8226\'20\'22 Japanese importers still have plenty of buying to do going forward \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY's 30/60-day log correlations with EUR/JPY are above +0.50 \u8226\'20\'22 The two \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs close in the same direction more often than not GBP/USD eyes last week's low as UST yields rise further \u8226\'20\'22 Cable hits 1.3131 after extending south from 1.3179 (Asian session high) \u8226\'20\'22 1.3131 is 10 pips shy of last week's low (March 22, after hawkish Powell) \u8226\'20\'22 Drop to 1.3131 influenced by further rise in UST yields: 2-year up to 2.41% \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 Citi\uc2  expects 50 bps Fed hikes in May, June, July and Sept \u8226\'20\'22 1.3160 (Friday's low) is now a GBP/USD resistance level \u8226\'20\'22 Locked Russian assets add to war woes for British pension funds AUD/USD scales fresh five-month peak as Aussie soars vs yen \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD reaches 0.7540 (new five-month high) as AUD/JPY soars to 94 \u8226\'20\'22 94 is highest level for AUD/JPY since July 2015 (92 was Friday's high) \u8226\'20\'22 Yen weakness spurred by BoJ move to tamp down bond yields \u8226\'20\'22 AUD supported by higher commodity prices, including iron ore \u8226\'20\'22 0.7555 (Oct high) and 0.76 are AUD/USD resistance levels beyond 0.7540 \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. 5-30 year yield curve inverts for first time since 2006 BoJ bond \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  speaks louder than words for yen Yen bears are having a very happy Monday as the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep bond yields pinned down overrides \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jawboning from Japan's government. USD/JPY soared to 125, its highest level since August 2015, and is set for its biggest one-day rise since March 2020, after the \uc2 BoJ\uc2  made two offers in a single day to buy Japanese Government Bonds at 0.25%. The BoJ's action has eclipsed comments from Japan's Chief Cabinet Secretary \uc2 Hirokazu Matsuno\uc2 , who said "it's desirable to for exchange rates to move stably, reflecting economic fundamentals". Friday's CFTC data, meanwhile, showed IMM speculators upped gross JPY short positions by 20% to 115,158 contracts in the week ended March 22, raising the net JPY short position to its biggest since January. USD/JPY bull targets include 130 and 135 (USD/JPY was last at those levels in 2002). {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/36SBDAh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/36SBDAh}}}\uc2  The rand and the Reserve Bank - a happy partnership The rand's 11.5% appreciation since November has helped the \uc2 South African Reserve Bank\uc2  in its inflation battle, and the relationship is likely to hold as the SARB adopts a more hawkish stance. The SARB could take its foot off the rate hike pedal given the rand's moderating influence on imported inflation but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone will not prevent South African inflation from testing the topside of the official 3% to 6% target range. Supply side pressures are being exacerbated by the Ukraine crisis with rising commodity and energy prices driving global inflation rates higher. South Africa's domestic energy woes are also contributing to the country's uncertain growth and inflation outlook. Second round effects including longer-term higher inflation expectations and wage growth will keep the SARB on a tightening bias, helping underpin the rand. Analysts expect rate hikes in May, July and September. The rand starts the week on a weaker footing versus the dollar but USD/ZAR's gains are not convincing and new trend lows are calling while rate hikes and rising commodity prices are in the mix. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3uxhPdZ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3uxhPdZ}}}\uc2  To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-3-31T10:54:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220328ei3s01q9p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
