\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen tumbles as \uc2 BOJ \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to keep bond yields pinned down\b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 08:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG/TOKYO, March 28 (Reuters) - The Japanese yen slipped nearly 1% to a six-year low on Monday, after the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop government bond yields from rising above its key target, while rising U.S. yields pushed the dollar higher against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  too.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2 , which has repeatedly said it is committed to keeping monetary policy loose, on Monday made two offers to buy an unlimited amount of government bonds with maturities of more than five years and up to 10 years. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aiming to stop rising global interest rates from pushing up Japanese yields.\par \par \uc2 The dollar climbed roughly 0.95% to 123.25 yen, its highest since December 2015. It rallied over 7% so far in March, its biggest monthly gain in over five years.\par \par \uc2 "The market sees monetary policy divergence between the U.S. and Japan as the key driver of dollar-yen, so in contrast to the hawkish Fed comments recently, the (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 's action) gives the impression that the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  remains dovish, and that's leading to a higher dollar-yen," said Shinichiro Kadota, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "I think the risk is still to the upside in the near term, especially if this monetary policy divergence story stays intact. But the speed has been quite fast and it does seem a little overheated, so if we see any contrary headlines, we could see some correction as well," he added.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year Treasuries yield was last 2.5567%, its highest since May 2019, and up 6.5 basis points on the day, as traders position themselves for an aggressive series of rate hikes from the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The two year yield was 2.412%, its highest since April 2019, with these higher rates underpinning the dollar. The greenback index against a basket of major rivals advanced 0.38% to 99.194.\par \par \uc2 The euro slid 0.27% to $1.0950 and sterling lost 0.36% to $1.3150.\par \par \uc2 "We expect that the euro will remain heavy this week. The balance of risks suggests EUR/USD may test 1.0800 in coming weeks," said \uc2 CBA\uc2  analysts in a note.\par \par \uc2 Inflation figures from major European economies and the eurozone are due from Wednesday, which could also affect the direction of the euro.\par \par \uc2 Also potentially driving the dollar this week is Friday's non farm payroll data in the U.S., though given the market is already positioned for an aggressive pace of rate hikes this year, its effect could be muted say analysts.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie dollar bucked the trend however, inching higher to $0.7513 to hold near last week's four-month high, helped on the day by rising Australian bond yields, as well as the longer term impact of higher commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 Aussie \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers are also looking out to Australia's budget on Tuesday. Australia's Treasurer said on Sunday the budget would mark a very significant material improvement to the government\u8217\'20\'19s bottom line.\par \par \uc2 One possible headwind for the Aussie is the COVID-19 situation in China, after Shanghai said on Sunday it would lockdown the city to carry out COVID-19 testing.\par \par \uc2 The dollar climbed to a two week high of 6.3986 on the offshore yuan on Monday morning, before paring gains.\par \par \uc2 In cryptocurrency markets bitcoin was sitting pretty around $46,900 after jumping to as high as $47,766 in early trading, its highest level since early January.\par \par \uc2 Ether, the world's second largest cryptocurrency, was at $3,320.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Alun John in Hong Kong and Kevin Buckland in Tokyo; Editing by Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-3-28T07:44:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220328ei3s00weq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
