\par\par \b \uc2 French Government Bonds Seen Positively Amid Europe's Energy-Transition Need -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 08:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0730 GMT - \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  's rates strategists maintain a positive view on French government bonds, or OATs, in the eurozone government bond space, with one factor behind is France's less pronounced need for energy transition compared to other countries. "The war in Ukraine boosts support for [President Emmanuel] Macron in the presidential race, and France should be less impacted than other countries by a stepped-up energy transition," the strategists say. The 10-year OAT yield is trading 1.2 basis points higher at 0.985%, while the 10-year OAT-Bund yield spread is trading broadly unchanged at 45 bps, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . The first round of the French presidential elections will be held on April 10. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0723 GMT - USD/SGD edges lower in the afternoon Asian session but is likely to be supported by ongoing geopolitical risks that underpin the safe-haven appeal of the greenback. There are fears of Russia resorting to chemical or nuclear attacks, Maybank says, noting \uc2 NATO\uc2  leaders pledged overnight to ramp up defenses against the threat of chemical and nuclear weapon attacks from Russia. Markets appear to be awaiting the next catalyst from Russia-Ukraine developments, Maybank adds. USD/SGD is down 0.1% at 1.3562. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT - The Nikkei Stock Average closes 0.1% higher at 28149.84, led by gains in chemical and pharmaceutical stocks, despite weakness in banks and insurers. \uc2 Mitsubishi Chemical Holdings\uc2  gains 2.6% and Nippon Paint Holdings rises 2.5% as some falls in crude oil prices ease concerns about higher costs of raw materials. Investors remain focused on the war in Ukraine and its impact on commodity prices. Broader market index Topix ends flat at 1981.47. The 10-year Japanese government bond yield rises half a basis point to 0.235%. USD/JPY is at 121.81, compared with 122.37 as of Thursday 5 p.m. Eastern Time. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0602 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is likely to have diminishing returns, analysts say, noting yen-supportive comments from Finance Minister Suzuki and \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Governor Kuroda earlier today. "The more you do it, the less impact it tends to have," says \uc2 Jeffrey Halley\uc2  , senior market analyst at Oanda. "The MOF and \uc2 BOJ\uc2  are probably more concerned about the pace of the yen decline, rather than the decline itself," he adds. Also, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sell USD/JPY as this would "upset the U.S. for a start, and unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tends to be short-term gain, ending in the same pain," he adds. USD/JPY is down 0.6% at 121.67. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0528 GMT - \uc2 Westpac\uc2  turns more hawkish about the trajectory of \uc2 RBA\uc2  raising its official cash rate. If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  starts to increase rates in August this year, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  now expects that by December 2022 the \uc2 RBA\uc2  would have restored the 65 basis points of emergency cuts which it implemented during Covid-19. \uc2 Westpac\uc2  had earlier projected that this would not happen until February 2023. Even though the \uc2 RBA\uc2  is set to run policy according to Australia's economic conditions rather than following the U.S. Fed, the more aggressive actions of the Fed are likely to slightly accelerate the \uc2 RBA\uc2  's tightening profile, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0335 GMT - JPY strengthens against G-10 and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on possible demand by Japanese companies to repatriate profits on foreign holdings to Japan for accounting purposes ahead of the country's fiscal year-end on March 31. This demand appears to be supporting JPY against USD, SPI Asset Management says. However, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  's accommodative monetary-policy stance compared with the tightening bias among a number of countries such as the U.S. may help to limit JPY's gains, analysts say. USD/JPY falls 0.5% to 121.72, SGD/JPY drops 0.5% to 89.73 and AUD/JPY is down 0.4% at 91.54. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT - \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  has trimmed its Australian 2022 GDP growth forecast to 4.2% from 4.7%, saying a recent drop in consumer confidence amid cost-of-living pressures will dent growth. Still, robust household spending will be a key driver of activity. A combination of restricted spending opportunities and generous government compensation for workers through pandemic lockdowns has left household balance sheets in great shape, with a build up of A$250 billion of extra household savings. As a result, consumers seem ready and willing to spend, says \uc2 David Plank\uc2  , ANZ's Head of Australian Economics. A 6.5% on quarter surge in household consumption in 4Q and higher retail sales activity in January suggests spending is going to remain strong. (james.glynn@wsj.com ; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0036 GMT - South Korea's Kospi edges down 0.1% to 2728.06 in mixed early trade. Battery stocks advance while airline shares retreat. Wall Street's rebound overnight supports sentiment but the upbeat mood is capped by concerns over higher inflation and supply shortages. North Korea's latest test of an intercontinental ballistic missile is also stoking geopolitical risks while the war in Ukraine continues weighing on investors' minds. USD/KRW is 0.2% higher at 1,221.10 on risk aversion. Index heavyweight \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  is 0.1% lower. Battery maker \uc2 LG Energy Solution\uc2  gains 4.4%. Budget airline \uc2 Jeju Air\uc2  falls 1.2%.(kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT - Japanese stocks may rise as the yen weakens sharply overnight despite continuing uncertainty over the war in Ukraine and its impact on global trade. Nikkei futures open up 240 points at 28065 on the SGX. USD/JPY is at 122.38 after hitting 122.42 earlier, its highest level since December 2015, compared with 121.44 as of Thursday's Tokyo stock market close. EUR/JPY rises to 134.73, its highest level since Feb 2018. Investors remain focused on the war in Ukraine and its impact on commodity prices. The Nikkei Stock Average closed 0.3% higher at 28110.39 on Thursday. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2051 GMT - The IPC stock index closes up 1.2% at a record high 55830 points and the peso strengthens to 20.09 against the US dollar from 20.22 yesterday as the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  raises its policy interest rate by 50bp to 6.5%, in line with expectations. Statistics institute Inegi reports mid-March CPI at 7.29%, practically unchanged from February, while core CPI rose to 6.68% from 6.59%. January retail sales were up 0.6% from December, but nonfinancial services fell 2%. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2049 GMT - Chemical trade groups are urging federal maritime regulators to investigate ocean carriers' "frequent unreasonable refusals" to ship hazardous materials, according to a letter. Companies represented by members of the Council of Chemical Association Executives are facing cancellations, rejections, months-long delays and escalating costs for shipping dangerous goods, the group writes to the \uc2 Federal Maritime Commission\uc2  . The organization calls the carriers' snubs a potential violation of US shipping law, and says the disruptions are adding to already mounting inflation and strains in the supply of critical materials. The request comes as the \uc2 FMC\uc2  is looking into complaints by agriculture exporters that container lines are rejecting their shipments. (lydia.oneal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2032 GMT - While the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  's 50bp increase in its policy interest rate to 6.5% was widely anticipated, the tone of the statement was "harder than expected," Vector Casa de Bolsa says. The unanimous vote sends a signal of concern even for one board member who had previously favored smaller rate increases. "Given the complicated inflationary context, we expect the reference rate to be at least 8% by the end of the year," Vector adds. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its inflation forecasts for 2022 and 2023, with risks to the estimates biased to the upside. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 25, 2022 03:30 ET (07:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220325ei3p000kz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
