\par\par \b \uc2 German Companies' Hiring Expectations Fall in December -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 2021\par 08:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0756 GMT - German companies are less willing to hire employees than in the previous month, the \uc2 Ifo Institute\uc2  says. The Ifo employment barometer fell to 102.9 points in December from 103.9 points in November. Due to the current wave of the coronavirus, three industries have announced layoffs: hospitality, events and tourism, Ifo says. In contrast, the employment barometer rose again in manufacturing, where companies are planning to take on more staff next year to handle bulging order books. Service providers have revised their personnel plans significantly downward, with consumer-related services even foreseeing layoffs. In retail, hiring has come to an almost complete stop, Ifo says. Inconstruction, there is a slight tendency to increase the number of employees, according to the \uc2 Ifo Institute\uc2  . (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT - Eurozone government bond yields are expected to rise in 2022, with the 10-year Bund yield likely ending next year in positive territory, \uc2 Generali Investments\uc2  says. The combination of increasing U.S. yields, an expected adjustment of long-term key rate expectations, less support from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  , and an economy still growing above potential will contribute to a rise in long-term yields, senior bond strategist Florian Spaete says. Forwards imply a slight increase of 10-year Bund yields to -0.25%, but \uc2 Generali Investments\uc2  forecast the end-2022 level at 0.10%, he says. The 10-year Bund yield is trading 0.9 basis point higher at -0.364%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0710 GMT - The clarity provided by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  regarding the asset purchase reduction in 2022 is a surprise, Ostrum Asset Management says. "A surprise is the visibility provided, as the \uc2 ECB\uc2  has accurately described the path of QE throughout 2022," Ostrum AM says. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  confirmed the scheduled end of the Pandemic Emergency Purchase Programme at end-March, and to help offset this, it will increase the regular Asset Purchase Programme in 2Q and 3Q, though this will still mean a reduction in overall purchases. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  purchases will absorb eurozone countries' net bond issuance in 2022, Ostrum AM says, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will limit tensions in sovereign rates and spreads. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0700 GMT - Eurozone noncore government bond yield spreads are still rather low by historical standards and they look vulnerable to a shift in the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's narrative next year, Florian Spaete, senior bond strategist at \uc2 Generali Investments\uc2  , says. "The tighter \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity will dampen the current carry-friendly low volatility environment," while an expected increase in core yields will reduce the search for yield, he says. However, solid growth, still accommodative monetary policy and the support from the NextGenerationEU program will prevent a disorderly increase in risk premiums, he says, forecasting moderate spread widening in 2022. Presidential elections in Italy and France have the potential to have a lasting impact on the current calm political environment, Spaete says, expecting it to become bumpier in 2022. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0640 GMT - China is likely to take more steps to limit the fallout for its debt-riddled property sector, but this doesn't mean it will rescue all troubled developers, \uc2 UOB\uc2  reckons. "While China's property sector, taken as a whole, is of systemic importance to the overall economy, individual property developers would not be considered systematically important," the Singapore bank says. It estimates property developers and related sectors, including heavy-equipment and building-material suppliers, have debt equivalent to 35% of China's financial system or 100% of China's GDP. Should signs of contagion grow, \uc2 UOB\uc2  expects state-owned banks will find a way to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (yongchang.chin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0619 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Nikkei Stock Average climbed 2.1% to close at 28517.59 as U.S. stock futures rose and the yen weakened. Equities in Asia including Japan are higher, thanks to a wave of short-covering sharply lifting U.S. stock futures, Oanda says. Top performers on the Nikkei included \uc2 Lasertec\uc2 , rising 4.9%, and \uc2 Advantest\uc2 , gaining 4.6%. \uc2 Shionogi & Co\uc2  . jumped 5.3% after saying Monday that its oral drug has high antiviral activity against Covid-19's Omicron variant. Meanwhile, Kobe Bussan lost 0.9% and Daifuku shed 0.5%. USD/JPY was at 113.74, compared with 113.44 at Monday's Tokyo stock market close. The yield on the 10-year Japanese government bond was up 1.5 basis points at 0.050%. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have little room for a further cut in the loan prime rate if it wants to maintain stability in the banking sector, \uc2 Nomura\uc2  says. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  lowered the one-year loan prime rate to 3.80% from 3.85% on Monday. Any more LPR cuts will inevitably squeeze the net interest margin of commercial banks and potentially hurt their profitability, the Japanese investment bank says. Chinese lenders are already set to be hit by slower growth in the overall economy and in particular by the slumping property sector, it adds. (clarence.leong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0302 GMT - The move by \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut its one-year loan prime rate to 3.80% from 3.85% "sent a signal of Beijing's easing bias, but its real impact will be quite limited," \uc2 Nomura\uc2  says. The Japanese bank says Monday's cut is "too small to be impactful," and that the five-year LPR--to which most mortgage loans are benchmarked--was kept unchanged, showing that Beijing doesn't intend to stimulate the property sector. The cut may not effectively address the "real drags" on the Chinese economy, which include the supply-side shock due to the Winter Olympics, rising costs of China's zero-Covid strategy and slowing export growth, it says.(clarence.leong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are mixed against USD in the morning Asian session as investors weigh conflicting signals. Gains in regional equity markets are likely to underpin appetite for risky assets. However, any outperformance of this week's U.S. core PCE price index data could substantiate a more hawkish shift from the Fed, particularly in the form of an earlier rate increase, which might translate into a tailwind for USD, IG says. The index gauges consumer prices of goods and services, excluding food and energy. USD/KRW rises 0.2% to 1,191.86 while USD/SGD is little changed at 1.3666 and AUD/USD edges 0.1% higher to 0.7119. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0020 GMT - Japan's Nikkei Stock Average climbs 1.6% to 28389.92 as U.S. stock futures rise and the yen weakens. News of Monday's enactment of an extra budget to fund the Japanese government's economic stimulus package aimed at mitigating the effects of Covid-19 also seems to be supporting the domestic stock market. A mixed bag of companies lead gains, with \uc2 Shionogi & Co\uc2  . adding 6.4%, \uc2 Advantest\uc2  rising 3.1% and \uc2 Lasertec\uc2  up 3.3%. Meanwhile, \uc2 Hoya Corp\uc2  . loses 1.0% and \uc2 Z Holdings\uc2  is down 1.0%. USD/JPY is at 113.65 compared with 113.44 at Monday's Tokyo stock market close. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2235 GMT - Australian consumer confidence nudged higher again last week despite rising numbers of Covid-19 cases. The ANZ Roy Morgan survey showed a 0.4% rise for its third straight weekly increase. ANZ Senior Economist Adelaide Timbrell says an apparently lower rate of hospitalizations from the Omicron Covid-19 variant compared with the previously dominant Delta strain probably helped. Confidence in the most populous states of New South Wales and Victoria rose, but the increase was moderated by a pullback in Queensland, South Australia and Western Australia. Weekly inflation expectations rose by 0.3 percentage points to 5%, above its four-week moving average of 4.9%. (stuart.condie@wsj.com; @StuartLCondie)\par \par \uc2 1922 GMT - Traffic to US stores rose 19% on the last Saturday before Christmas, compared to last year, according to data from Sensormatic Solutions, which tracks footsteps with cameras placed at stores. Foot traffic was down 26% from 2019. Traffic to stores was 28% lower on Black Friday compared to 2019, but up 48% from 2020. So-called Super Saturday is expected to be the second busiest day of the holiday shopping season. (charity.scott@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 21, 2021 02:56 ET (07:56 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020211221ehcl000h0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
