8226\'20\'22 0.7536 was Wednesday's high, after hot Australian CPI data Yen view from BoJ chief lessens "black swan" risk The probability of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly ordering the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to strengthen the yen has fallen after comments from BoJ Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  on Thursday. Kuroda said a softer yen is "definitely positive" for the Japanese economy and that the benefits of a weak JPY, such as a boost to Japanese exports, outweigh the damage from increased import costs spurred by its recent fall. Earlier this month, Japan's Prime Minister \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2  issued a rare warning over the potential disadvantages of a weak yen. Kuroda's sanguine views about the weaker yen may be welcomed by Japanese insurers who have grown increasingly reluctant to buy foreign government bonds without a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedge due to them not expecting the recent USD/JPY rally to continue for much longer. The last time the BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was in 2011, when it sold the yen to counter a spike prompted by devastating earthquakes and the subsequent Fukushima nuclear crisis. EUR/USD likely to be pinned by huge cluster of expiries Despite the event risk of the looming \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting, EUR/USD will likely remain pinned near to 1.1600 for the rest of the week because of a large cluster of option expiries. EUR/USD edged lower ahead of the \uc2 ECB\uc2  meeting as investors waited to hear policymakers' views on the outlook for inflation and an expected pushback against rising interest rate projections. There is a huge amount of option expiries that are containing EUR/USD in the 1.1590-1.1610 region worth 2.9 billion euros, rolling off on Thursday and Friday. This will leave spot free to move on Monday, as there are relatively few expiries in the vicinity. Last week EUR/USD's recovery move has been limited by the key 1.1670 Fibonacci level, a 38.2% retrace of the 1.1909 to 1.1522 (September to October) drop, putting spot under stress that could retest the October 1.1522 low. Only a daily close above the 1.1670 Fibo would shift the bias back to the upside. To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-10-31T11:19:14.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020211028ehas02iqp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
