UD/USD is settled around 0.7535/40 \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD getting support by elevated Aus bond yields and risk on mood \u8226\'20\'22 The Aus 10-year yield is 16 BPs higher than the US 10-year yield \u8226\'20\'22 Key resistance is eyed at 0.7555/65 with sellers ahead of 0.7550 \u8226\'20\'22 The 50% of 2021/high/low and the 200-day MA converge at 0.7555/65 \u8226\'20\'22 Support is at former resistance at 0.7475/80 \u8226\'20\'22 Trend higher is gaining momentum, so too soon to pick a top GBP/USD-Private sector pay to rise - cable targets 1.3900 \u8226\'20\'22 Trades +0.05% at the top of a tight 1.3822-1.3830 range with moderate flow \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to raise rates to 0.25% in Q1, or sooner poll \u8226\'20\'22 UK pay awards set to regain pre-pandemic pace - XpertHR \u8226\'20\'22 Private sector employers expect 2.5% pay increases to Aug 22 - no huge jump \u8226\'20\'22 Charts; 5, 10 & 21 day moving averages climb, 21 day Bollinger bands expand \u8226\'20\'22 Daily momentum studies head north - positive signals continue to build \u8226\'20\'22 Targets 1.3831 break, 50% of 2021 fall, and test of 1.3913 September high \u8226\'20\'22 Close below 1.3705 rising 10 DMA needed to undermine the topside bias EUR/USD correction to test 1.1725, as the USD skids The EUR/USD bounce has entered its second week, led by a weaker U.S. dollar, and has further to run technically. In the bigger picture, this is a healthy correction in the primary downtrend. The outlook for interest rate differentials remains supportive of the USD in the longer term. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is on track to taper this year despite moderating U.S. economic growth, as inflationary and wages pressures build. In Europe, inflationary pressure is seen as transitory and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is about to lose its most prominent hawk. Yet there are short-term negatives for U.S. growth expectations and potentially the USD. Supply chain issues, a major contributor to inflationary pressures, appear set to persist for longer than initially thought. While the market is confident that President Joe Biden will deliver a stimulus package, uncertainty over its size, composition and timing could undermine confidence. Technically the short-term EUR/USD outlook is positive, though the primary downtrend since May remains in place. Since mid-July, EUR/USD has swung between the upper and lower 21-day Bollinger bands four times, pivoting around the 21-day moving average. EUR/USD rejected the lower band in early October and closed above the 21-DMA on Tuesday. The break was sustained on Wednesday, suggesting that the next move is a test of the falling upper band, at 1.1726 on Thursday. Little threat of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  action until 'Kuroda ceiling' Speculation that the Japanese government and \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  are contemplating \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb recent JPY weakness looks premature. Tokyo players are not expecting any action until USD/JPY trades up through 120 and towards the 124-125 area that attracted mention from BOJ Governor Kuroda in June 2015. Recent comments from Japanese officials have caused some USD/JPY bulls to tread more carefully. A senior cabinet official noted on Wednesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability is important and the government is watching the (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) market carefully. Finance Minister Shunichi Suzuki said the same on Oct 15. These comments should be taken at face value and not extrapolated as hints of displeasure or verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at reversing the USD/JPY trend. They were in response to questions from the media, and the government's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not changed. The argument is being made that the weaker yen, supply chain disruptions and recent surge in imports, will limit Japan's economic recovery. Yet these recent trends will also boost inflation, which remains a goal for the government and BOJ. Indeed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  targets 2% inflation versus actual readings around zero for some time. The Kishida government, like the Suga and Abe administrations before, is pro-business. And Japan Inc appears content to see JPY weaken further to boost exports despite higher raw material prices and supply disruptions which will eventually be resolved.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-10-24T04:50:45.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020211021ehal00kzl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
