15-week peak of 0.7505 in Asia \u8226\'20\'22 Rise to 0.7505 influenced by higher Australian government bond yields \u8226\'20\'22 Investors expect RBA cash rate hikes well before 2024 \u8226\'20\'22 AUD shorts squeezed hard during climb from 0.7226 over the past fortnight \u8226\'20\'22 Net AUD short held by IMM speculators hit all-time high in week ended Oct 5 \u8226\'20\'22 0.7534 (July 7 high) is a resistance level beyond 0.7505 Japanese action would speak louder than words on yen With its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jawboning seemingly falling on deaf ears, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to bite the bullet and order the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buy yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if it wants to alter the weak JPY trend. On Wednesday, shortly after the yen fell to a four-year low against the U.S. dollar, with 114.69 marking the USD/JPY EBS high, Japan's deputy chief cabinet secretary Yoshihiko Isozaki said "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability is extremely important, so we'll continue to watch market moves carefully". Isozaki's comment echoed last Friday's steer from finance minister Shunichi Suzuki, which itself came days after premier Fumio Kishida issued a rare warning over the potential disadvantages of a weak yen. BoJ Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  may have something to say about the yen at his briefing following next week's BoJ meeting (Oct. 27-28). Friday's CFTC data showed IMM speculators raised gross JPY short positions by 18% to 112,444 contracts in the week ended Oct. 12, lifting the net JPY short to a 29-month high. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3jiehI2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3jiehI2}}}\uc2  EUR/USD could be stuck close to 1.15-18 all year Traders are betting EUR/USD falls but clearly the results of the break below the 1.16-19 range have disappointed them, with profit-taking lifting it back to what may well be the middle of a new 1.15-18 range that could hold for the rest of the year. Techs are the mainstay of bearish expectations, so the loss of momentum is a big issue for those short who will make next to nothing from interest rates during any consolidation phase. Despite a rise in U.S. yields and expectations the Fed will soon start tapering, there has been no change in the differentials between German and U.S. bond yields that are the basis for EUR/USD. There is virtually no carry and there has been no change to support a EUR/USD decline since June's \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  meeting. That said, the euro is undermined by negative interest rates and with stimulus driving positive trends throughout markets and weighing on funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the euro, it shouldn't rise far. The monthly Ichimoku cloud at 1.1448 is the likely base of a new range, while highs shouldn't stretch far beyond the minimum tech target to correct losses since June's Fed, which is 1.1754. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2XwCJ0w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2XwCJ0w}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3G25GCW"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3G25GCW}}}\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-10-23T10:50:02.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020211020ehak01u7d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
