\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  may  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to arrest yen rise but only once, says former  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  tsar\b0\par\par\uc2 313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Dezember 2020\par 07:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 24 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent the yen from rising above 100 to the dollar, but just once given the difficulty of keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates pegged at a set level, former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe\uc2  said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts expect the yen, which hovered around 103.50 to the dollar on Thursday, to rise past the psychologically crucial 100 mark in coming months as expectations of huge U.S. fiscal spending weigh on the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Watanabe, who oversaw Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy from 2004 to 2007, said he did not expect the yen to shoot up much as narrowing interest-rate differentials between the United States and Japan keep the dollar/yen in a tight range.\par \par \uc2 If the yen were to climb past 100 to the dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step into the market under pressure from Prime Minister \uc2 Yoshihide Suga\uc2 , who is known for his aversion to yen rises that hurt Japan's export-reliant economy, he said.\par \par \uc2 "There's a chance the MOF will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  just once to defend the 100 line, mostly as a political gesture," Watanabe, who retains close contact with incumbent bureaucrats, told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot keep the dollar sustainably above 100 yen in the massive global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, he added.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may work for two to three days if done in a big enough scale," Watanabe said. "But you can't stop \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves on a permanent basis with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2011, when the March 11 earthquake and tsunami crippled the economy and sent the yen soaring to a record around 75 per dollar. (Reporting by Leika Kihara and Yoshifumi Takemoto; Editing by Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-12-24T07:34:13.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020201224egco00mj5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
