\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan's Suga says stable  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  moves 'extremely important'\b0\par\par\uc2 356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2020\par 03:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Japan will keep in close contact with other nations on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  - Suga\par \par \uc2 * Remarks echo those of BOJ Governor Kuroda\par \par \uc2 * Analysts see 100 yen vs dollar as line-in-the-sand (Recasts with Suga's comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara and Kaori Kaneko\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 6 (Reuters) - Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Yoshihide Suga\uc2  on Friday vowed to work closely with overseas authorities to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves stable, signalling his readiness to respond to any yen spike that threatens to derail the country's fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 "Exchange-rate stability is extremely important," Suga told parliament, when asked how Japan will respond to any changes a new U.S. administration could make to its dollar policy.\par \par \uc2 "We will respond appropriately on markets, while keeping in close contact with overseas authorities," Suga said. He declined to comment on specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels or moves.\par \par \uc2 Suga's remarks followed those by Bank of Japan Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , who said on Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will work closely with financial authorities to help keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves stable.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to 103.59 yen in Asia on Friday, close to an eight-month low, as a contentious U.S. presidential election diminished hopes for large stimulus to support the economy any time soon.\par \par \uc2 A yen spike has historically been a trigger for monetary easing by the BOJ and jawboning from authorities keen to prevent yen rises from hurting Japan's export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts see 100 yen to the dollar as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's line-in-the-sand. But the hurdle for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is high, as Tokyo is unlikely to win consent from other countries also suffering from the hit to their economies from the coronavirus pandemic, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Some market players expect the BOJ to deepen negative interest rates if the yen spikes, though that option is also controversial given the strain years of ultra-low rates are inflicting on financial institutions' profits. (Reporting by Leika Kihara and Kaori Kaneko Editing by Chang-Ran Kim and Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-11-6T03:13:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020201106egb600el1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
