\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan to appoint Okamura as top financial diplomat - sources\b0\par\par\uc2 411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2020\par 10:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, background)\par \par \uc2 By Takaya Yamaguchi and Yoshifumi Takemoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 10 (Reuters) - Japan is set to appoint international policy veteran Kenji Okamura as its top financial diplomat representing the country at international forums such as G7/G20 meetings, five government sources with knowledge of the matter told Reuters on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Okamura, 58, currently international bureau chief at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), will replace \uc2 Yoshiki Takeuchi\uc2 , who will retire this month after serving in his post for one year in a mid-year personnel reshuffle, the sources said on condition of anonymity as they are not authorised to speak to media.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's cabinet will decide on the personnel change next week, which will take effect as early as July 20, they said.\par \par \uc2 The appointment of Okamura comes at a delicate time when Japan needs to closely coordinate with the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  advanced nations and \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  major economies to respond to the global downturn wrought by the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Okamura is expected to fill the bill given his experience as the MOF international organisations division chief in 2008-2010 spearheading Japan's efforts to battle the global financial crisis by coordinating with G7/G20 and \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Okamura's duty as vice finance minister for international affairs would include overseeing any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary to prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-led economy from slipping deeper into a recession.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since the aftermath of the 2011 earthquake and nuclear crisis, although authorities tend to verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem spikes in the yen that risk hurting the export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 Takeuchi has served in his post for just one year, a relatively short period compared with some of his predecessors.\par \par \uc2 Takeuchi's predecessor \uc2 Masatsugu Asakawa\uc2 , now the head of \uc2 Asian Development Bank\uc2  (ADB), served for four years, the longest serving financial diplomat. Asakawa's term exceeded the previous record set by Bank of Japan Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , who was vice finance minister for international affairs for 3-1/2 years to January 2003.\par \par \uc2 The government will also name Mitsuru Ota, 60, as vice finance minister - the top bureaucrat at the finance ministry - to replace Shigeaki Okamoto who is retiring, the sources said. (Reporting by Takaya Yamaguchi and Yoshifumi Takemoto; Writing by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Jacqueline Wong, William Maclean) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-7-10T09:20:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200710eg7a010yl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
