h Friday now seen cap for now but USD still "king" \u8226\'20\'22 Lower US yields and Nikkei downtrend, AXJ equities fall keep risk mood sour \u8226\'20\'22 Option expiries help cap too, @USD1 bln @111.00, USD3.4 bln 111.50-112.00 \u8226\'20\'22 Tech support around top of hourly Ichi cloud, 55-HMA, both ascending \u8226\'20\'22 Despite USD/JPY volatility, little chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  EUR/USD-Settling around 1.0715 after whippy morning session \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD is trading higher at 1.0715 - up 0.30% from the NY close \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD traded down to 1.0636 in early Asia in thin market conditions \u8226\'20\'22 A brutal day for equities with the AXJ index and S&P futures falling 4.0% \u8226\'20\'22 More government shut-downs as coronavirus spreads spooking markets \u8226\'20\'22 Failure of \uc2 US Senate\uc2  to pass aid package on schedule also weighed on risk \u8226\'20\'22 EUR and JPY moved higher against risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the AUD and NZD \u8226\'20\'22 A break above 1.0735 in the EUR/USD targets Friday's high at 1.0831 \u8226\'20\'22 Support formed around 1.0630 and buyers tipped ahead of 1.0600 \u8226\'20\'22 Volatility likely to continue and accelerate if US doesn't pass aid package AUD/USD-Weak with risk assets but off early lows \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD gapped lower to 0.5780 at the open after weekend of negative news \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD filled in gap and traded to 0.5825 in very thin market \u8226\'20\'22 News US Senate couldn't yet agree on aid package sent S&P futures down 5% \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD slid down to 0.5695 before fresh buying emerged \u8226\'20\'22 The AUD/USD recovered when the USD broadly weakened as US yield eased \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD is trading around 0.5750 heading into the afternoon \u8226\'20\'22 Price action in AUD/USD is expected to remain volatile \u8226\'20\'22 If US doesn't pass aid package markets well sell off and weigh on AUD \u8226\'20\'22 Support at 61.8 of 0.5510/0,5986 move at 0.5692 \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance and sellers at Friday's higher at 0.5986 GBP/USD-Choppy, as US delays stimulus, coronavirus dominates \u8226\'20\'22 -0.2% after will they won't they on the US stimulus package, expected Monday \u8226\'20\'22 Moderate volumes with little liquidity early, and risk under pressure \u8226\'20\'22 UK scientists to track mutations in coronavirus to map spread \u8226\'20\'22 Coronavirus and potential impact on the global economy dominates sentiment \u8226\'20\'22 Charts; 5, 10 & 21 daily weekly and monthly MAs trend south - negative setup \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling found support below 1.1500 last week, easing over sold signals \u8226\'20\'22 USD remains a significant factor, and continues to trend higher \u8226\'20\'22 Asian 1.1536-1.1740 range is initial support-resistance USD gains likely if US lawmakers fail to make deal The USD has made strong gains since the coronavirus crisis intensified and started to infect credit markets, forcing investors to flee into cash. The USD was broadly supported by disturbingly tight conditions in USD funding markets, as well as U.S. investors selling foreign holdings and repatriating funds. The central banks have gone to great lengths to address stress in USD funding markets and there were decent recoveries in some risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday/Friday when conditions eased. News that U.S. Senate Republicans and Democrats couldn't agree on a massive stimulus bill sent S&P futures tumbling 5.0% to hit limit-down early Monday in Asia. Reports indicate that both sides are continuing to talk, but confidence a deal can be agreed to before Wall Street opens Monday has been shaken. If a deal isn't announced by then, there is a risk of a scary meltdown in risk assets. While the central banks may have fixed the plumbing in funding markets, it won't stop U.S. investors liquidating what they have left in foreign holdings and non-U.S. investors rushing into safe-haven US dollars. The =USD is down around 0.35% at 102.45/50 after failing to break 103.00 for the third straight day. Failure to reach a deal by the time Wall Street opens later today will likely see =USD take out 103.00 and start making tracks for the January 2017 and 17-year high at 103.82. USD/JPY to remain volatile but no BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  USD/JPY trading remains extremely volatile but Tokyo players see little chance of any MOF-ordered BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now with the pair well above its March 9 101.18 low and below 115 or 120, considered areas of caution by Japanese players. Traders initially long the pair were forced to bail when it slid to 101.18. Then shorts were forced to cover as it zoomed back up to as high as 111.50 Friday. Recent moves have also played havoc with Japanese institutional investor portfolios and especially those who sold USD on the way down to hedge positions. Like shorter-term players, some in this bloc have since been forced to pare hedges with U.S. yields jumping and helping USD/JPY to its Friday high. Option players have also been affected by recent sharp moves, with volatility spiking and many players whipsawed. What is now certain is USD is king amidst the coronavirus spread. That said, some sales were seen in Asia today with USD/JPY down to 110.08 from 111.26 after a large U.S. coronavirus spending bill got held up in the Senate. Japanese exporters were likely in the mix of sellers but perhaps more from options players. Massive expiries are eyed today around 111.00 (total @$1 bln) with more between 111.50-112.00 (total $3.4 bln). The ascending hourly Ichimoku cloud between 109.06-110.30 and 55-HMA at 110.12 currently look to work as cushions for now. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation may linger if swaps can't slow dollar Anyone hoping that an all-out assault on dollar funding issues would stop the relentless rise in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has yet to be satisfied, leaving some to wonder whether concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a weapon that might eventually be considered. Even after escalating steps from the Fed to address the funding issue through swaps, which helped narrow the EUR 3-month cross \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basis swaps dramatically by Friday's close from extremes hit earlier in the week, it did not prevent EUR/USD from making new lows. "The idea is that the provision of those dollars will satisfy the demand, which arguably was amplifying the disruption," said \uc2 Marc Chandler\uc2  at Bannockburn Global \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But, he added, "Unlike during the financial crisis, the problem now is not counter-party risk but the access to cash dollars." That thirst for dollars could also increase as the fallout from the coronavirus crisis spreads through the global corporate sector. "If swaps do not work to ease the pressure, additional steps on the escalation ladder may be necessary," said Chandler, noting that "the Reagan-Volcker" and "Clinton-Rubin" dollar rallies ended with coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But, he added: "Unlike the previous experience, other major central banks do not appear nearly as concerned about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness."\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-3-26T04:32:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200323eg3n00kix\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
