\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-Japan is stuck with curse of safe-haven yen\b0\par\par\uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 02:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.)\par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, March 11 (Reuters Breakingviews) - Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  will struggle to lift the curse of a safe-haven yen. Market turmoil has sparked a stampede into refuges, such as gold, high-rated government bonds and the yen. While a sustained rally will hammer exporters, there may be little that policymakers in Tokyo can do about it.\par \par \uc2 The yen\u8217\'20\'19s initial reaction to the coronavirus outbreak in China was to weaken, not least because of the trading ties between the two countries. But that\u8217\'20\'19s changed as the virus has spread globally and after Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  unexpectedly cut the U.S. policy interest rate last week. The latest lurch higher in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came on Monday when tumbling oil prices sent global markets into a tailspin. The resulting stampede into safe havens saw the yen appreciate to its strongest against the dollar since 2016.\par \par \uc2 That erodes the international competitiveness of export-dependent heavyweights such as Hitachi, \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Panasonic\uc2 , and explains why Bank of Japan chief \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  said on Tuesday he would respond to any economic damage that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might inflict. His options are, however, limited given policy rates are already below zero, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying assets for years.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by selling yen for dollars. Such solo action is unlikely to work, however, and will irk other major industrial nations. \uc2 OECD\uc2  data puts the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s fair value in purchasing power parity terms at 103 per dollar. Given Japan already meets two of the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s three criteria for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation, open \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would tick the third box. It might also trigger a tit-for-tat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation in China.\par \par \uc2 Another option is stealth action by the Government Pension Investment Fund, which manages $1.6 trillion of assets and could sell yen to finance purchases of overseas assets. But even the GPIF\u8217\'20\'19s best efforts may flop given daily trading volumes in the foreign exchanges were a massive $6.6 trillion, according to the most recent \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2  data. What would help is calmer markets since this would dull the yen\u8217\'20\'19s safe haven allure. That is out of Abe and Kuroda\u8217\'20\'19s hands. On \uc2 Twitter{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/petesweeneypro"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/petesweeneypro}}}\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS\par \par \uc2 - The Japanese yen strengthened as much as 11% against the dollar between Feb. 20 and March 9 as investors sought out safe havens. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which fell as low as 101.2 yen on March 9, had firmed to trade around 105 yen in morning trade on March 11.\par \par \uc2 - The U.S. dollar weakened against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the same period, partly because of a surprise March 3 decision by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut the target federal funds rate by 50 basis points to a range of 1.00% to 1.25%.\par \par \uc2 - Bank of Japan Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  said on March 10 he would respond as needed to any adverse impact that recent yen appreciation could have on the economy and prices, although he added that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe BOJ\u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy does not directly target \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 -The \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s next scheduled policy meeting will be held on March 18-19.\par \par \uc2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can click on\par \par \uc2 - SIGN UP FOR BREAKINGVIEWS EMAIL ALERTS: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bit.ly/BVsubscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://bit.ly/BVsubscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Swaha Pattanaik and Jamie Lo) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-3-11T02:19:45.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200311eg3b0063h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
