\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-COMMENT- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  may be a necessary evil\b0\par\par\uc2 174 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 14:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Financial markets are in crisis and the United States, which is going to great lengths to support them, needs all the help it can get. Were \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize JPY and prop up its own stock market, the United States would probably support those moves. If not, it's become clear that this crisis could get much worse. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a crisis is not manipulation of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain an advantage. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and provide investors with time to adjust is surely prudent. Change is inevitable, but rapid changes are bad. It's possible that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now could move JPY much further than it did in the past. A lot of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows once ended up on bank balance sheets. In effect, counter trading \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That won't happen now. Those executing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will report back the impact precisely. With the full order being executed, the impact should be greater.\par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-3-13T13:28:20.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200310eg3a02iw9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
