\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Financial Times\uc2 : Coronavirus crisis could lead to new  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  war\b0\par\par\uc2 873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The world had more than ten years since the 2008 financial crisis to prepare for another global recession. But he didn't make good use of that time. There has now been a great shock in the form of a coronavirus outbreak, at a time when the world's largest economies are divided by trade disputes. There is real danger of an uncoordinated political response that will extend economic weakness and provoke a new round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars.\par \par \uc2 There is still much unknown about the disease known as Covid-19. However, what is known indicates that the only effective response, once the virus is installed, is total quarantine as happened in China and now in Italy. What is inevitable is the great global economic shock that comes with it. The spread of the outbreak is partly responsible for the sharp fall this week by global stock prices and bond yields.\par \par \uc2 In an ideal world, each \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would face this challenge with a healthy level of inflation and interest rates above 5%. If Covid-19 hit domestic demand, they would cut rates as needed. Governments would provide specific tax assistance. There could be some exchange rate swings, depending on which countries are most affected, but there would be little reason for global economic tension.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, that is not the current situation. In much of the rich world, particularly in the eurozone and Japan, interest rates are below zero and the use of other relief tools, such as asset buying, is close to the limit. In the Us, official rates began this crisis in a range of 1.5% to 1.75%, but have already dropped by 50 basis points. Countries like Mexico, with interest rates of 7%, and Russia with 6% have greater leeway.\par \par \uc2 This asymmetry is a recipe for what former Brazilian Economy Minister Guido Mantega called "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars": it is not a simple exchange manipulation in search of competitive advantages, but of divergences in monetary policy that provoke sudden movements of exchange rates and threats of retaliation by those who lose out.\par \par \uc2 If the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  lowers interest rates when the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  cannot follow it, the dollar is likely to weaken against the euro and yen. The Fed's emergency cut last week, and the subsequent leak to safer assets, reached the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 101 yen per dollar. Levels close to 100 yen are bad for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exports and elsewhere led to threats of monetary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yen could be further strengthened if the US country cuts rates to zero. The euro is in the same situation.\par \par \uc2 The Fed also has limited scope to lower the rate further, and even if the dollar falls against the yen, it is rising against the ruble, Mexican peso, Indian rupee and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially those affected by Saudi Arabia's decision to extract more oil and next fall in crude oil prices. At least for now, it appears that the coronavirus crisis is causing a leak to U.S. security and not a capital outflow to higher-yielding emerging markets, as happened in 2010 when Mantega issued the warning.\par \par \uc2 The crucial question is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will do in an election year with the U.S. economy under pressure. Will \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation be tolerated against a number of North American trading partners? It doesn't seem to be in his way. Trump might well resort to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or punitive tariffs sometime this year if he thought the country's trading partners are benefiting. Theglobal economic cooperation is already fragile; either of those two movements would completely fracture it.As monetary policy room for manoeuvre is limited, the alternative is fiscal policy. But countries also differ in their willingness to incur deficits. The eurozone, and Germany in particular, is often reluctant to raise spending. Fiscal policy is therefore also fraught with potential international tensions. Let's imagine that the UK applies a great fiscal stimulus in response to the coronavirus, but the \uc2 EU\uc2  does not. European exports could benefit from demand in Britain, and The already difficult Brexit trade negotiations would become much more complicated.\par \par \uc2 The only answer is global cooperation. Japan and the eurozone may not be able to cut their interest rates to match the Fed, but the U.S. softening should be done with them. \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2 , the fed president, went to great lengths to cooperate with G7 central banks ahead of their rate cut last week. While their efforts do not lead to coordinated global rate cuts, it remains crucial that central banks' unity is maintained. The Federal should expand \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap lines with other central banks to help them meet demand for dollars if they lack local liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Although the medical landscape is getting dark, there are still grounds for economic hope. Unlike the 2008 subprime mortgage crisis, the shock does not come from the financial sector, making it less destabilizing and more likely to pick up a rapid pick-up. But there is only one way to achieve this: in the economy, as in the direct fight against the disease, the world must work together.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020200311eg3a000mi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
