\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Japan is ready to act on excessive yen volatility as virus spreads\b0\par\par\uc2 519 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 10:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Spread of new virus outbreak prompts risk-aversion, boosts yen\par \par \uc2 * Policymakers fear rapid yen gains damage export-led growth\par \par \uc2 * Aso declines comment on whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market\par \par \uc2 * Watching market with urgency as 'nervous moves' seen - \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  tsar (Recasts throughout with context on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 9 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will respond appropriately when necessary to curb excessive volatility in the yen in accordance with G7-G20 agreements, its top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat said on Monday, stepping up a warning against rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Yoshiki Takeuchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, made the remark after an ad-hoc meeting of government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials, as the yen hit 101.58 per dollar, its highest in three years.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials say they are sticking to an agreement of the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  and \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  economies that excessive volatility and disorderly market moves damage economies, a tacit agreement they interpret as allowing action against sharp market swings.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Nikkei share index closed down 5.1% at 19,698.76 on Monday, amid mounting fears the spread of the coronavirus could severely damage the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "Nervous moves have been continuing in the market. We will respond as appropriate when necessary in line with the G7-G20 accords," Takeuchi told reporters after a meeting with his counterparts from the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and the financial watchdog.\par \par \uc2 "We'll closely monitor market moves with a greater sense of urgency than before."\par \par \uc2 Takeuchi's latest warning underscores Japanese policymakers' concerns that any sharp yen appreciation hurts competitiveness of the country's exports and could weigh on the export-led economy, which is teetering on the edge of recession.\par \par \uc2 Japanese policymakers tend to talk down gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and step up warnings as yen rises accelerate.\par \par \uc2 Expressions such as recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves being one-sided, watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves with grave concern, or being ready to act "decisively" against sharp yen spikes are seen as a prelude to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some market players see rapid yen gains beyond 100 yen and towards 95 yen as the trigger for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, therefore, there is still some room before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they say.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stayed out of the market since 2011 when it heavily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem yen gains in the wake of the Fukushima nuclear disaster triggered by large earthquakes and a tsunami.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  declined to comment when asked earlier whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  needed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the yen's current strength.\par \par \uc2 The spread of the coronavirus epidemic has prompted heavy selling of riskier assets by investors and a scramble into assets such as the yen, which are perceived to be safer havens during times of financial distress.\par \par \uc2 The number of people infected with the coronavirus topped 107,000 across the world and 3,600 people have died, as the outbreak caused more economic disruption. (Editing by Kim Coghill and Jacqueline Wong) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-3-9T10:08:54.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200309eg39003xq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
