\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei Closes Up 1.6%; Lifted by Consumer, Electronics Stocks -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2020\par 07:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0618 GMT - The Nikkei Stock Average closed up 1.6% at 23575.72, lifted by consumer and electronic stocks. \uc2 Kose Corporation\uc2  was up 5.0%, \uc2 Hisamitsu Pharmaceutical\uc2  gained 4.5% and \uc2 Takara Holdings\uc2  was 3.9% higher. Among electronics stocks, \uc2 Olympus\uc2  closed up 4.1%, \uc2 NEC Corp\uc2  . gained 3.6% and \uc2 Casio Computer\uc2  was 2.9% higher. The market will continue to track developments in the Middle East. Although these headlines may persist, the lack of concrete escalation for now could limit downside for the USD-JPY, putting the pair back within the wide 107.80-109.80 range, \uc2 OCBC\uc2  says. The USD/JPY is at 108.41, compared with 108.31 as of Monday's Tokyo stock market close. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0557 GMT - The sharp rise in global oil prices after the U.S. airstrike that killed an Iranian military leader in Baghdad is likely to fuel China's inflation, says ANZ Research. Higher oil prices have led to a supply shock in China, which coupled with pricier pork would raise headline inflation, the research group says. However, the oil shock is unlikely to change the monetary-policy stance of the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  , which is expected to keep an easing bias, says ANZ. It maintains its forecast for another 50 bps reduction in the reserve-requirement ratio for large banks this year. ANZ also expects China to set a growth target of about 6% for 2020. (yiwei.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0500 GMT - Indonesia's plan to cut corporate tax rates in 2020 "could be a game changer," and may dramatically boost investment and lift annual GDP growth above 5.5% in the coming years, \uc2 UOB Kay Hian\uc2  says. The brokerage notes that reductions in the country's corporate tax a decade ago boosted total investment by an average of 26% a year in the 2008-2013 period. \uc2 UOB Kay Hian\uc2  is "cautiously bullish" about Southeast Asia's largest economy in 2020, and believes a new 20% tax rate -- down from 25% currently -- will drive higher investment and result in higher household consumption. Among its top stock picks this year are \uc2 Astra International\uc2  , \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  , \uc2 Bank Negara Indonesia\uc2  and IndoFood Sukses Makmur. (ben.otto@wsj.com; @benottoWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0310 GMT - USD/CNH is poised to form a "death cross" chart pattern, Maybank says. Both the 21-day moving average and the 50-day moving average are at the brink of crossing below the 200-day moving average, which would be bearish for the pair. USD/CNH is also testing the 200-day moving average and a Fibonacci level at 6.9620, which is 61.8% retracement of the July-to-September rally. Subsequent support is at 6.9060. Resistance levels are at 7.0070 and 7.0515. However, a break of the 200-day moving average may not be that easy, given prevailing risk-off climate, Maybank says. USD/CNH last down 0.1% at 6.9608. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0309 GMT - Singapore's economy could stage a modest recovery in 2020 if the global manufacturing cycle improves and trade tensions ease with the signing of the phase-one trade deal in January, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says. Manufacturing accounts for about 20% of Singapore's economy and has contracted for four straight quarters, due to waning global demand for electronics amid the trade spat, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says. This partly contributed to Singapore's 0.7% GDP growth in 2019, the slowest since 2009, it adds, noting \uc2 Singapore's Ministry of Trade and Industry\uc2  has a GDP growth forecast of 0.5%-2.5% for this year. (martin.mou@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0250 GMT - \uc2 Singapore government\uc2  bonds are expected to appeal to insurance companies and asset managers, ANZ says. On-year growth in insurance firms' assets has rebounded to 11%-12% in 2019 after falling to 8%-9% in 2018. Debt holdings in their portfolios have also risen to around 54% currently, from a low of 51.8% in late 2017. Excluding U.S. Treasuries, \uc2 Singapore government\uc2  bonds offer attractive yields to asset managers, compared with other debt instruments rated AAA, ANZ says. Gross issuance of government bonds is expected to reach S$23.4 billion in 2020, compared with S$25.0 billion in 2019. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT - Any significant ramp-up in U.S.-Iran tensions could push AUD/USD below 0.6900, \uc2 CBA\uc2  says. However, two factors may keep the pair in a tight range for now. On one hand, risk of disruption to Middle East oil supplies weighs on AUD as oil price spikes hurt global economic growth. This growth is a major driver of commodity prices, and by extension AUD. On other hand, risk of disruption to Middle East oil supplies supports AUD because Australia exports gas. Oil and gas are close substitutes, and their prices move in tandem, \uc2 CBA\uc2  says. AUD/USD last down 0.1% at 0.6932. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT - Japanese stocks may move higher tracking the U.S. stocks which pared their declines and rose modestly overnight. Nikkei futures open up 195 points at 23305 on the SGX. Investors are likely to continue weighing the prospect of fresh conflict across the Middle East after the U.S. strike in Iraq. The USD/JPY is at 108.37, compared with 108.31 as of Monday's Tokyo stock market close. The Nikkei Stock Average ended down 1.9% at 23204.86 on Monday. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2253 GMT - \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  cautions against using the impact of the 2009 Black Saturday bushfires as a yardstick for the jolt to the economy delivered by this year's devastating fire season. It notes the Black Saturday fires burned 450,000 hectares of land in a shorter timeframe with an estimated cost of A$4.4B. In contrast, it says more than 6M hectares of land has been ravaged this fire season. "We expect to see evidence of this disruption in Dec/Jan retail sales, and in the 4Q/1Q GDP release (particularly travel spending)," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says. Sentiment surveys should give an early read on magnitude. "The consequent rebuilding and insurance payouts are likely to modestly boost economic growth later in the year," the bank adds. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2056 GMT - The benchmark IPC stock index closes down 0.3% at 44495, with bellwether \uc2 America Movil\uc2  giving up 1.3% and cement maker \uc2 Cemex\uc2  off 0.3%. \uc2 Coca-Cola Femsa\uc2  declines 0.5%. The company plans to issue global notes and use proceeds to repurchase its $900M in 3.875% notes due 2023. The peso strengthens in Mexico City at 18.85 to the US dollar, compared with 18.92 Friday. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2151 GMT - The AUD/EUR falls to a 3-week low of 0.6198 as better-than-expected eurozone \uc2 PMI\uc2  data buoys EUR bulls. Data provider \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  raised December's services \uc2 PMI\uc2  to 52.8 from its flash estimate of 52.4, representing an improvement on 51.9 in November. A reading above 50 signals expansion in sector activity. The final December composite \uc2 PMI\uc2 , which measures services and manufacturing activity, was also revised up to 50.9 from 50.6. "Still, more evidence of subdued Eurozone inflation pressures can curtail EUR/USD upside and support AUD/EUR," \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  says. It expects the eurozone December headline CPI inflation print, due today, to track the \uc2 ECB\uc2  's 2019 forecast of 1.2%. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1615 GMT - The Japanese yen should outperform the Swiss franc on any escalation in geopolitical concerns, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. The yen and the franc, which are considered safe haven assets, are the best-performing G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last Thursday as U.S. -Iran tensions grow, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  's \uc2 Jane Foley\uc2  says. Persistent gains in these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  "will not be welcomed by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  or the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  ," she says. The policies of the two central banks are "positioned to reduce the appeal" of speculative inflows into their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  goes "one step further" than \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  by threatening \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That means the outlook for JPY/CHF would be "moderately higher" on increased geopolitical worries. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 07, 2020 01:18 ET (06:18 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200107eg17000ak\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
