\par\par \b \uc2 Japanese Stocks May Move Higher -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2020\par 00:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1844 ET - Japanese stocks may move higher tracking the U.S. stocks which pared their declines and rose modestly overnight. Nikkei futures open up 195 points at 23305 on the SGX. Investors are likely to continue weighing the prospect of fresh conflict across the Middle East after the U.S. strike in Iraq. The USD/JPY is at 108.37, compared with 108.31 as of Monday's Tokyo stock market close. The Nikkei Stock Average ended down 1.9% at 23204.86 on Monday. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1753 ET - \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  cautions against using the impact of the 2009 Black Saturday bushfires as a yardstick for the jolt to the economy delivered by this year's devastating fire season. It notes the Black Saturday fires burned 450,000 hectares of land in a shorter timeframe with an estimated cost of A$4.4B. In contrast, it says more than 6M hectares of land has been ravaged this fire season. "We expect to see evidence of this disruption in Dec/Jan retail sales, and in the 4Q/1Q GDP release (particularly travel spending)," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says. Sentiment surveys should give an early read on magnitude. "The consequent rebuilding and insurance payouts are likely to modestly boost economic growth later in the year," the bank adds. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1656 ET - The benchmark IPC stock index closes down 0.3% at 44495, with bellwether \uc2 America Movil\uc2  giving up 1.3% and cement maker \uc2 Cemex\uc2  off 0.3%. \uc2 Coca-Cola Femsa\uc2  declines 0.5%. The company plans to issue global notes and use proceeds to repurchase its $900M in 3.875% notes due 2023. The peso strengthens in Mexico City at 18.85 to the US dollar, compared with 18.92 Friday. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1651 ET - The AUD/EUR falls to a 3-week low of 0.6198 as better-than-expected eurozone \uc2 PMI\uc2  data buoys EUR bulls. Data provider \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  raised December's services \uc2 PMI\uc2  to 52.8 from its flash estimate of 52.4, representing an improvement on 51.9 in November. A reading above 50 signals expansion in sector activity. The final December composite \uc2 PMI\uc2 , which measures services and manufacturing activity, was also revised up to 50.9 from 50.6. "Still, more evidence of subdued Eurozone inflation pressures can curtail EUR/USD upside and support AUD/EUR," \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  says. It expects the eurozone December headline CPI inflation print, due today, to track the \uc2 ECB\uc2  's 2019 forecast of 1.2%. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1115 ET - The Japanese yen should outperform the Swiss franc on any escalation in geopolitical concerns, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. The yen and the franc, which are considered safe haven assets, are the best-performing G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last Thursday as U.S. -Iran tensions grow, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  's \uc2 Jane Foley\uc2  says. Persistent gains in these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  "will not be welcomed by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  or the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  ," she says. The policies of the two central banks are "positioned to reduce the appeal" of speculative inflows into their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  goes "one step further" than \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  by threatening \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That means the outlook for JPY/CHF would be "moderately higher" on increased geopolitical worries. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1111 ET - The U.K. economy is likely to take a hit from its "ill-advised" plan to leave the EU , says \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  . The investment bank expects the country's GDP to rise by an average of 1.3% this year, but to fade thereafter. Investment strategist Sebastian Raedler says the U.K. is removing itself from the most advantageous trading arrangement it could have with the EU by leaving the bloc. "Withdrawing from a mutually beneficial arrangement is ill-advised," he says. "GDP might be lower by 6-7% in the long run because you're choosing a sub-optimal model." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1110 ET - Spain's long road to forming a government could come to a provisional conclusion in Tuesday's parliamentary ballot, but still the future of the socialist PSOE and left-wing UP alliance is uncertain without its own majority, leaving Spain politically unstable, says \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2  . The ERC party, which aspires to Catalonia's independence, will have to abstain from the vote to enable a simple majority in the upcoming, second vote. The end of the political vacuum and the less rigid attitude of coalition members toward Catalonia would be positive from a market perspective, but the risk of early failure of the coalition remains high in view of the limited room for manoeuver, he says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1104 ET - The euro is likely to remain stuck in a $1.110-$1.1250 range for the time being as the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kicks off a review of its monetary policy strategy, \uc2 BMO Capital Markets\uc2  says. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  's inflation target is one of the key areas of its strategic review starting January, meaning investors "should look through upside misses" on core consumer price inflation data, BMO \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Stephen Gallo\uc2  says. "Though alterations to the \uc2 ECB\uc2  's reaction function could further inflame intra-euro political tensions, we can't help but think that [Fabio] Schnabel's appointment to the executive board will initially push the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the direction of tolerating higher rates of inflation for longer." EUR/USD rises 0.3% to 1.1188.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1103 ET - The Turkish lira is likely to fall further against the dollar in coming months as risk appetite weakens on escalating U.S.-Iran tensions, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. The U.S. killing of Iranian general \uc2 Qassem Soleimani\uc2  Friday, provides "little reassurance that a risk-off scenario can be excluded in the near-term," \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analysts say in a note. An acceleration in Turkish inflation in December also "does not bode well for the lira" while monetary policy measures by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are "not having any effect whatsoever" on curbing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's depreciation. USD/TRY is last flat on the day at 5.9725. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1048 ET - Debt investors should not pin expectations for tightening credit spreads exclusively on corporate bond purchases by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as its effect "seems to be waning," say ABN AMRO 's fixed-income strategists. "While we expect an upscale in the level of purchases from April onwards, we would not bank on spread tightening purely from this event," they say. "We get a feeling that the technical support no longer has meaningful impact on spreads as it did when it [the corporate bond purchases program] was first announced back in early 2016." While \uc2 ECB\uc2  's bond purchases may have lost their power pushing spreads lower, a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  presence "does keep spreads anchored," they say.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1004 ET - Securities written to provide insurance on the \uc2 US government\uc2  's creditworthiness may be sending a misleading message, \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  research says. Starting in 2014, the volume of activity essentially dropped out of the quotes for US credit-default swaps, the analysis says. "Quoted CDS spreads in this market are based on few, if any, transactions," the research says. "Are the quoted US sovereign CDS spreads a useful measure of market participants' expectations of US sovereign credit risk? ... This is unlikely to be the case," the bank adds. The paper's finding is useful because it suggests the next time US CDS moves around some budget brinkmanship, it may not be sending a real read on what markets think about the risks around government borrowing and debt service. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0947 ET - Despite a mixed macro picture and tensions in the Middle East, euro-denominated corporate debt issuance is poised "to start with a bang" in January after the lull Christmas period, says \uc2 ABN AMRO\uc2  . "European firms have been facing a significant funding gap, which had not been made up by new net issuance in the 2019," says the bank. This, coupled with central banks' easing monetary policies keeping "funding conditions at decent levels," is likely to unleash more new bond sales, it says.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 06, 2020 18:44 ET (23:44 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200106eg16003q4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
