entary and Analysis Massive options and major Fibo - hurdle for EUR/USD bulls \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD new post Fed recovery high - trades 1.1103-55 Monday \u8226\'20\'22 US 10yr yield 3yr low 1.746, closes gap vs 10yr DE 232.30 vs 253 last week \u8226\'20\'22 Huge option expiries loom this week - delta hedging may help draw/contain \u8226\'20\'22 1-billion 1.1100 Tues, but 4-billion 1.1100-25 Thursday is main focus \u8226\'20\'22 Bulls must overcome big resistance at pre Fed highs 30-31 July 1.1161-62 \u8226\'20\'22 Also 38.2% Fibo retracement of 1.1412-1.1027 at 1.1174 USD/JPY biggest weekly drop of 2019 raises Japan's fears \u8226\'20\'22 Last week USD/JPY closed down 207 pips, the biggest one-week drop of 2019 \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY options flag the risk - a very volatile ride ahead \u8226\'20\'22 Japan signals it will act to curb any excessive yen rises \u8226\'20\'22 There is a 107.75 NY cut expiry worth 1.4bn which could exert magnetism \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY has seen a 105.79-106.68 range Mon, according to prices on the EBS \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY range has been 117.68-118.44. Previous USD/JPY update Pound elicits support from UK service PMI beat \u8226\'20\'22 GBP rises after UK service PMI beat, 51.4 vs 50.2 forecast \u8226\'20\'22 Cable up to 1.2155 vs intra-day low just shy of 1.2100 pre-UK data release \u8226\'20\'22 Drop to threaten 1.2100 was fuelled by risk aversion \u8226\'20\'22 UK shares plunge as trade worries fuel selloff \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/GBP retreats to 0.9173 vs pre-UK service PMI high of 0.9200 \u8226\'20\'22 0.9200 = highest level for EUR/GBP since Sept 2017 AUD/USD feels CNH heat pre RBA, options high alert for more losses \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD hits 12th consecutive lower daily low at 0.6748 Mon \u8226\'20\'22 Eyes Jan. 3 flash crash low at 0.6715 as markets await RBA Tues \u8226\'20\'22 USD/CNH above 7.00 hurts AUD, RBA unlikely to cut before Oct/Nov RBAWATCH \u8226\'20\'22 Volatility/downside risk to AUD from further CNH weakness, risk aversion \u8226\'20\'22 Option vols spike - O/n 17.0 - break-even 50 pips (23 pips on a normal day) \u8226\'20\'22 1-month vol adds 1.0 vol since Fri, 2.0 vols since FOMC - big move \u8226\'20\'22 Risk reversals also see massive spike for AUD puts - downside protection Major U.S bank sees 0.9400 potential for USD/CHF In its trade idea of the week, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  likes to sell USD/CHF at market, with a target of 0.9400 and a stop at 0.9900. They see carry trades undermined by three factors: U.S monetary tightness caused by an estimated $157 billion of Treasury bill issuance in August, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raising the bar for further rate cuts and U.S/China trade tensions. As carry trades are liquidated, the U.S yield curve should invert further. MS recognises the inflows will support the dollar, but franc appreciation should dominate. A further inversion of U.S yields should put pressure on the Fed to rethink its policy as monetary conditions tighten. That would weaken the dollar, which would become the main driver of this short USD/CHF trade. The bank thinks that any \uc2 Swiss National Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will only slow the USD/CHF decline, not reverse it. The risk to the trade is a significant rally in risk assets. USD/CHF is already off from 0.9845 to 0.9761 Monday, from 0.9975 on Aug. 1. Chart: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2MFIG3w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2MFIG3w}}}\uc2  Heat in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  kitchen gets close to boiling point The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is on alert for an expected response from U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to China's decision to let the yuan breach the key 7-per-dollar level for the first time in more than a decade on Monday. China's move inflames the U.S/China trade conflict, and has spurred fresh demand for the safe-haven Swiss franc and Japanese yen -- with EUR/CHF falling to a 26-month low of 1.0864 and USD/JPY dropping to a seven-month low of 105.79 (EBS). Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat responded to Monday's yen appreciation by signalling that Tokyo was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the stronger yen hurts \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2011. If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  orders the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to weaken the yen, it might complicate relations with Trump as he fights with China over trade -- speculation has been growing that Trump might himself order \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the dollar at some point. Chart: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2MDNE0l"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2MDNE0l}}}\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-8T11:11:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190805ef8501rcl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
