\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Japan signals it will act to curb any excessive yen rises\b0\par\par\uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2019\par 08:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Tokyo will respond if yen rises hurt economy - Takeuchi\par \par \uc2 * Top \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat also warns excess volatility undesirable\par \par \uc2 * MOF, BOJ, FSA talks discussed market impact of trade woes (Adds background on Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Daniel Leussink and Yoshifumi Takemoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 5 (Reuters) - Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat on Monday warned investors against significantly pushing up the yen, signalling that Tokyo was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if excessive yen gains threatened to hurt the export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 Yoshiki Takeuchi, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, said Tokyo was in regular contact with overseas authorities to respond to volatile market moves.\par \par \uc2 "As we have been saying, it's necessary to take action based on the G7 and G20 agreement if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves have a negative impact on the economy and financial markets," Takeuchi told reporters, when asked whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem excessive yen rises.\par \par \uc2 He made the comments after a meeting of top officials from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and the Financial Services Agency, which is held occasionally to discuss market developments.\par \par \uc2 The meeting discussed how Sino-U.S. trade tensions and developments in U.S. monetary policy may have given rise to nervous moves in the market, Takeuchi said.\par \par \uc2 "On the other hand, we were able to confirm the global and the Japanese economies were in a stable situation," he said.\par \par \uc2 Asian shares slid to 6-1/2-month lows on Monday and the yuan slumped to a more than decade trough as a rapid escalation in the Sino-U.S. trade war sent investors stampeding to traditional safe harbours including the yen, bonds and gold.\par \par \uc2 The flight to safety lifted the yen, which often gains at time of stress thanks to Japan's position as the world's largest creditor. The dollar slipped to a seven-month low of 105.78 yen.\par \par \uc2 Japanese policymakers tend to jawbone markets when the yen rises, as they fear a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hurt the country's exports and business sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo has stayed away from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since 2011 when it heavily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem a yen surge in the wake of a devastating earthquake and the Fukushima nuclear disaster.\par \par \uc2 Japan has stuck to the G7/G20 agreement against competitive devaluation, with a pledge to promote market-oriented exchange rates and use monetary policy to achieve domestic objectives without targeting \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Daniel Leussink and Yoshifumi Takemoto, Writing by Leika Kihara Editing by Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-5T07:55:55.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190805ef8500s0d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
