\par\par \b \uc2 In stagnant Japan, \uc2 ECB\uc2  policymakers catch glimpse of their own future\b0\par\par\uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2019\par 11:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Euro zone faces risk of Japanification\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 ECB\uc2  shelves tightening plans\par \par \uc2 * BOJ's policy may be hard to replicate\par \par \uc2 By Francesco Canepa and Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 FUKUOKA, Japan, June 9 (Reuters) - Europe's top central bankers who met their global peers in Japan this weekend may have caught a glimpse of their own future.\par \par \uc2 With a stagnant economy and declining population for the past two decades, Japan has long been seen as a harbinger for the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 Talk of the "Japanification" of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc is set to gain further traction after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  last week shelved plans to raise interest rates from record lows and opened the door to ease policy further.\par \par \uc2 That evoked parallels with the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's prolonged stimulus policy under Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , which has seen it gobble up 45 percent of the country's government debt as it tries to hit an elusive 2 percent inflation target.\par \par \uc2 "There's a real risk of Japanification right now," one euro zone \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said at the meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in Fukuoka, southern Japan.\par \par \uc2 The similarities are obvious. Both Japanese and euro zone governments have been slow in implementing politically costly but much-needed reforms to boost productivity, such as making their labour markets more flexible.\par \par \uc2 This has created a negative loop whereby low borrowing costs allow loss-making "zombie" firms to survive, locking up capital that could be used by more competitive ones, curbing productivity growth and perpetuating the need for low interest rates, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 CARBON COPY?\par \par \uc2 Whether that means monetary policy could be carbon-copied is less clear. Unlike the BOJ, the \uc2 ECB\uc2  must seek consensus among a group of diverse national central banks.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, the BOJ can keep printing money to keep borrowing costs ultra-loose under the administration of Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 , which is supportive of its pro-growth policies.\par \par \uc2 The prospect of an endless supply of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  money to finance government debt, however, is a hard sell for Germany - the most powerful economy in the euro area.\par \par \uc2 Following the BOJ's lead in buying stock index funds and other risky assets would also likely come under heavy resistance in the euro zone, where stocks are in hands of a healthy few.\par \par \uc2 Past experience showed lessons don't always travel well.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ took a leaf from Europe's book when it adopted negative interest rates in 2016. It took lessons from similar steps already put in place by central banks in Europe.\par \par \uc2 But the measure has been deeply unpopular among Japan's public and pushed long-term interest rates down too much, angering financial institutions which saw their margins crushed.\par \par \uc2 That made deepening negative rates among the least likely options even if the BOJ has to ease further, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ's lessons on quantitative easing aren't very promising either. Its massive debt purchases have dried up market liquidity and stoked concern the institution will run out of bonds to buy.\par \par \uc2 This is a predicament that will be all too familiar to \uc2 ECB\uc2  policymakers who face the additional challenge of respecting each country's quota when picking bonds to buy.\par \par \uc2 Still, some euro zone central bankers see the BOJ as a paragon of success for the mere fact that Kuroda's bold stimulus measures helped avert an economic downturn.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ is much more advanced than the \uc2 ECB\uc2  down the stimulus path and shows you can do even more quantitative easing without endangering financial stability," a euro zone central banker said.\par \par \uc2 "The situation in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be much worse had the BOJ not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as indeed it was before." (Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-6-9T10:27:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190609ef6900cf9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
