\par\par \b \uc2 Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Could  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  If Yen Rises Further: BNY Mellon -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 145 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Januar 2019\par 03:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0917 ET - With the Japanese yen suddenly jumping to a nine-month high versus the U.S. dollar overnight on Thursday, implied volatility went up in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Were the yen to rise even further, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, says \uc2 Neil Mellor\uc2 , senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNY Mellon. "In view of the uncertainty surrounding the health of the Japanese economy, yen strength is hardly consistent with the Bank's mandates. It is therefore perfectly possible that the BOJ - guided by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - could consider returning to direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Mr. Mellor says. The dollar is last down by 1% at JPY107.75, having fallen to a nine-month low of 104.79. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 04-01-19 0253GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020190104ef140002h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
