\par\par \b \uc2 Signs Emerge of Slowing Global Growth: \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2018\par 13:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0803 ET - Even before considering prospects of a looming trade war, there are "warning signs" that the global economy may be slowing, Paul Mortimer-Lee, chief market economist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 , says. The prospect of even higher readings for indicators such as purchasing managers' indexes is "relatively limited," leaving risks "largely to the downside." \b \highlight22\uc2 Central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stimulus is being withdrawn; "record US corporate buybacks" and "extended asset prices" reflect typical trends "near the tops of previous cycles," and slowing Korean semiconductor exports could signal "weaker global trade growth to come," he says. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0802 ET - Global political tensions could send USD/JPY to 100, given that the Japanese yen is seen as a safe-haven investment, and BNY Mellon thinks "it is perfectly possible that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could consider returning to direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market should renewed JPY strength emerge." USD/JPY fell to a 16-month low of 104.64 earlier on the back of U.S. tariffs on China, and the appointment of a new U.S. security adviser which may increase tensions between the U.S. and the Middle East. USD/JPY last trades down 0.2% at 105.09. Japanese authorities have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on 329 days in USD/JPY since 1991, according to BNY Mellon.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0724 ET - With tensions racheting higher in the U.S. tariffs issue, "analysts will be looking for signs of stability and compromise," says \uc2 Mihir Kapadia\uc2  , chief executive and founder of Sun Global Investments. The U.S. White House has already signalled that the EU and several other countries will be temporarility exempt from the steel and aluminium tariffs announced earlier, Kapadia says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0722 ET - John Bolton's appointment as the new U.S. national security adviser could be good for the euro if eurozone inflation rises due to an increase in oil prices and gives the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more confidence to tighten monetary policy, ING says. This could happen if the U.S. develops "a more aggressive stance to Iran" by having Mr. Bolton on the team, it says. Higher oil prices "may give the \uc2 ECB\uc2  greater confidence that the CPI is on a sustainable path to 2% and embolden the hawks," the Dutch bank says. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0712 ET - The dollar could fall as low as 103.80 against the Japanese yen on Friday "as risk assets tumble" on the back of U.S. President Donald Trump 's tariffs and appointment of John Bolton as the new national security adviser, according to ING. The tariffs, and the fact that Mr. Bolton is supportive of a more aggressive U.S. foreign policy, are likely to spark global political tensions. And since the U.S. suffers "a large net foreign liability position, the dollar looks exposed if the investment environment deteriorates," ING says. USD/JPY is down by 0.2% at 105.03, having dropped to a 16-month low of 104.64 earlier, according to Factset. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0648 ET - The Russian ruble's reaction to \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate cut by 25 basis points to 7.25% is muted, as most analysts had been predicting the move. USD/RUB falls slightly to 57.1907, from 57.2275 beforehand. The \uc2 CBR\uc2  said with prices growing in February by 2.2%, "inflation remains below 4% longer than expected, allowing for a quicker transition to neutral monetary policy this year." \uc2 CBR\uc2  Governor \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2  will hold a press conference at 1200 GMT. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0646 ET - Despite the uncertainties surrounding U.S. tariffs, UBS Wealth Management says its global tactical asset allocation remains pro-risk to benefit from still-strong global economic growth. It is important not to overstate the direct impact of U.S. tariffs on the global economy or equity markets at this stage, it says. The $50 billion represents only about 2% of total U.S. imports, while the tariffs will likely be on products than can be obtained outside of China to minimizeharm to the U.S. Still, UBS WM also holds counter-cyclical positions like overweight in 10-year U.S. Treasuries and overweight in the Japanese yen against the New Zealand dollar.(emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0639 ET - Uganda, Africa's top coffee exporter and an oil producer in the making, risks economic turmoil as mounting public debt poses a default risk, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says. Debt has more than doubled over the past three years reaching $15 billion--more than half of Uganda's GDP. Debt stood at around $6 billion three years ago, but Kampala continues to borrow heavily, notably from China to fund infrastructure projects, ahead of the planned commercialization of the country's vast oil fields, estimated to contain 6.5 billion barrels of crude. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warns that higher debt "may become a drag on economic growth by discouraging public investment due to the high debt service costs." (Nicholas.Bariyo@wsj.com;@Nicholasbariyo)\par \par \uc2 0622 ET - The Hong Kong interbank rate is showing signs of rising further, which ultimately should help the city's dollar against the greenback, says DBS economist Samuel Tse. The yield curve is steepening, showing expectations for rates to increase over the next year. Such an environment often helps a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value, all else equal. The Hong Kong dollar is just above the low end of its HK$7.85 trading band, at which the city's de-facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would act to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening further. Some investors have been pressuring the Hong Kong dollar after the authority's chief said it wouldn't issue debt to sop up liquidity like it did last year. That also occurred at a time of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakening. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0553 ET - Is India really insulated from US protectionist policies given its relatively small international trade? DBS doesn't think so. Even though the impact of US tariffs on steel and aluminium imports is likely to be limited since the US accounts for just about 10% of India's exports of these commodities, India could get hurt "if more blanket tariffs are imposed," DBS says. In services, the US runs a deficit. More than 60% of the revenue of Indian software firms comes from the US. Measures on services could hurt trade and push India's current account deficit back into trouble, DBS says. (anant.kala@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0550 ET - The trade war is a growing source of uncertainty for markets, say \uc2 KBC Bank\uc2  analysts but they think it won't hit the EUR/USD \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair's prevailing trading range for the time being. "For now we assume that the 1.2155-1.2555 trading range remains in place," \uc2 KBC\uc2  says. EUR/USD trades at 1.2323, up 0.2%. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0550 ET - Most of last month's gain in Singapore CPI came from higher food prices, which is "fickle, volatile and not a particularly good guide to domestic demand strength," says ING. In general, the data "ought to make us a little more comfortable in our forecast for a very-modest tightening" by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April. But "the likely transitory nature of some of this" doesn't comfort the bank. And then there's trade worries and the prospect of inflation softening some this month. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 23, 2018 08:03 ET (12:03 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180323ee3n001ht\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
