\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: Trade War Fears Boost Yen and Gold\b0\par\par\uc2 2,163 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2018\par 15:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's plans Thursday to levy up to $60 billion of tariffs on Chinese imports and his appointment of \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  as National Security Adviser spark fears of trade wars and Middle East tensions, prompting a hunt for safe havens. This lifts the Japanese yen, German bunds and gold, while the euro rises against the U.S. dollar. Not all moves follow the usual pattern though. Base metals and the Turkish lira suffers, but the Australian dollar--which usually falls in times of risk aversion--rises. The following is a selection of reactions and comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bond and commodity markets.\par \par \uc2 0754 GMT - Trade-war fears have so far left the safe-haven yen the biggest winner in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after Trump announced plans Thursday to levy up to $60 billion of tariffs on Chinese imports. That prompted retaliatory measures from China today. The dollar hit a 16-month low of Y104.64 earlier, and online \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  broker FXTM sees Y104 coming. "With uncertainty likely to heighten as trade tensions escalate, the yen may remain a risk-averse trader's best friend," it adds. The dollar is down 0.3% at Y104.92 and the euro is off 0.1% at Y129.32 after earlier hitting a 9-month low of Y128.96. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0810 GMT - As US trade tensions with China have risen further, RBC still thinks "China will opt to under-, rather than over-, react." The investment bank says it's more worried about National Security Adviser HR McMaster being replaced with \uc2 John Bolton\uc2 , "best known for being a chief architect of the Iraq war." The move "opens the way for a far-more-hawkish US foreign policy," says RBC. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0820 GMT - The potential for "a more aggressive stance" towards Iran after the appointments of "policy hawks" \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  and Mike Pompeo to U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's national security team could be bad news for the Turkish lira, which slid to record lows against the dollar and euro in overnight trade, says ING. USD/TRY last trades up 0.7% at 3.9634, having risen as high as 3.9889, according to FactSet. The prospect of rising Middle East tension due to "a hawkish White House" and higher oil prices "won't help the TRY," says ING, which also says USD/TRY could reach its 12-month forecast of 4.15 "much earlier." EUR/TRY is up 0.9% at 4.8847, having earlier reached 4.9677, according to FactSet. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 GMT - Eurozone periphery government bond spreads over German bunds trade slightly wider as the US administration's announcement of tariffs on Chinese imports has sent many investors into safe havens late Thursday, a trend that continues Friday. Spanish and Italian 10-year bond spreads trade 3 basis points wider at 79 basis points and 139 basis points, respectively, while Portuguese bond spreads are about four bps wider at 126 bps, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2 . That said, these widenings are contained. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0940 GMT - Recent Turkish lira depreciation could add about 1 percentage point to headline inflation in coming months, increasing the pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take action, says TEB, a unit of \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness isn't in line with macro economic fundamentals, Turkish President chief economic adviser Cemil Ertem Friday told state-run broadcaster TRT. USD/TRY is now at 3.9658 after the pair rose to a record high of 4.03, according to data provider Foreks. (yeliz.candemir@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0950 GMT - The trade war is a growing source of uncertainty for markets, say \uc2 KBC Bank\uc2  analysts but they think it won't hit the EUR/USD \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair's prevailing trading range for the time being. "For now we assume that the 1.2155-1.2555 trading range remains in place," KBC says. EUR/USD trades at 1.2323, up 0.2%. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1046 GMT - Despite the uncertainties surrounding U.S. tariffs, UBS Wealth Management says its global tactical asset allocation remains pro-risk to benefit from still-strong global economic growth. It is important not to overstate the direct impact of U.S. tariffs on the global economy or equity markets at this stage, it says. The $50 billion represents only about 2% of total U.S. imports, while the tariffs will likely be on products than can be obtained outside of China to minimizeharm to the U.S. Still, UBS WM also holds counter-cyclical positions like overweight in 10-year U.S. Treasuries and overweight in the Japanese yen against the New Zealand dollar.(emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1112 GMT - The dollar could fall as low as 103.80 against the Japanese yen on Friday "as risk assets tumble" on the back of U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's tariffs and appointment of \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  as the new national security adviser, according to ING. The tariffs, and the fact that Mr. Bolton is supportive of a more aggressive U.S. foreign policy, are likely to spark global political tensions. And since the U.S. suffers "a large net foreign liability position, the dollar looks exposed if the investment environment deteriorates," ING says. USD/JPY is down by 0.2% at 105.03, having dropped to a 16-month low of 104.64 earlier, according to Factset. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1122 GMT - \uc2 John Bolton\uc2 's appointment as the new U.S. national security adviser could be good for the euro if eurozone inflation rises due to an increase in oil prices and gives the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more confidence to tighten monetary policy, ING says. This could happen if the U.S. develops "a more aggressive stance to Iran" by having Mr. Bolton on the team, it says. Higher oil prices "may give the \uc2 ECB\uc2  greater confidence that the CPI is on a sustainable path to 2% and embolden the hawks," the Dutch bank says. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1202 GMT - Global political tensions could send USD/JPY to 100, given that the Japanese yen is seen as a safe-haven investment, and BNY Mellon thinks "it is perfectly possible that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could consider returning to direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market should renewed JPY strength emerge." USD/JPY fell to a 16-month low of 104.64 earlier on the back of U.S. tariffs on China, and the appointment of a new U.S. security adviser which may increase tensions between the U.S. and the Middle East. USD/JPY last trades down 0.2% at 105.09. Japanese authorities have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on 329 days in USD/JPY since 1991, according to BNY Mellon.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1206 GMT - Turkish government bond yields are trading slightly higher, except for that of the 10-year benchmark, as risk aversion sentiment prevails. Investors are tending to avoid or reduce holdings in riskier assets in the aftermath of U.S. tariff announcements and of the appointment of \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  as new U.S. security adviser which could result in increased tensions between the U.S. and the Middle East. Turkish bond yields trade higher by up to 4 basis points, according to FactSet. The biggest increase in yields--which rise as prices fall--affects the two-year bond which trades at a yield of 13.502%. Yield increases tend to be smaller towards the longer end of the Turkish curve. The yield on the 10-year bond trades 2 basis points lower at 12.247%. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1211 GMT - CEEMEA \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' resilience to the sell-off in global stocks shouldn't last for too long, given that "the Goldilocks scenario of synchronised global recovery is under serious threat from Trump's protectionist policies," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. Global growth is the reason why emerging markets have done well in the past couple of years. "It seems inevitable that at least some foreign investors will reduce their exposure to the CEEMEA assets, which in turn will weigh on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. Central and eastern European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have a reason to do well, though, given that the \uc2 EU\uc2  has been temporarily exempt from the U.S. tariffs on steel and aluminum. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1220 GMT - The view that the U.S. dollar would benefit from the U.S. trade protectionism and the fall in synchronized global growth is shared by \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . "Economic growth differentials favoring the U.S. economy, coupled with global growth weakening, creates a perfect environment for a USD rally," the bank says. Waning risk appetite should benefit the dollar, given that it is such a liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And "the U.S. economy may initially suffer less than its trading partners," such as the \uc2 EU\uc2 , which is a surplus-driven economy and trade tariffs may weigh on its exports, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1307 GMT - Gold is up 1.22% at $1347.02 a troy ounce on stronger haven appetite, with both base metals and the dollar hit by escalating fears of a trade war after the Trump administration implemented trade tariffs on steel and aluminum imports and promised more. The WSJ Dollar Index was recently down 0.3%, extending its five-day losses to 0.7%. A weaker dollar makes dollar-priced commodities less expensive for holders of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but that factor was outweighed by spiraling geopolitical risks and growing fears of slipping demand for metals. "All market moves today are some function of that news. Geopolitical risks have been taken a notch higher, and that's benefited gold prices," said \uc2 BMO Capital Markets\uc2 's Colin Hamilton. (david.hodari@wsj.com; @davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 23 Mar 2018 10:54 ET  Market Talk Roundup: Trade War Fears Boost Yen -2-\par \par \uc2 1310 GMT - Gold's ascent on Friday follows gains which came after this week's \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  interest rate guidance for 2018 stayed with three increases for the year instead of rising to four. However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did accelerate its schedule for rate increases in 2019 and 2020, and signaled an increased possibility that it could shift from three to four increases in 2018. A more hawkish interest rate policy might normally strengthen the dollar, but while "interest rates have gone up in recent months... the dollar's gone down and gold's kind of been caught in the middle," says Macquarie's \uc2 Matthew Turner\uc2 . Gold is up 1.22% at $1347.02 a troy ounce, with the dollar weakened by geopolitical risk after the Trump administration implemented trade tariffs and monetary policy so far this week. (david.hodari@wsj.com; @davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 1318 GMT - Copper prices are down 0.4% at $6697 a metric ton, with the industrial metals mostly lower and ferrous metals weighed down after the introduction of U.S. levies on Chinese steel and aluminum imports. "Concerns about future demand for industrial metals come as those tariffs may hurt demand. Base metals are down, but not by as much as ferrous metals, which are getting slammed because their focus on China," says \uc2 BMO Capital Markets\uc2 's Colin Hamilton. (david.hodari@wsj.com; @davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 1320 GMT - With China-U.S. trade tensions and U.S. monetary policy buoying gold today, political concerns are also supported by the continuing wave of departures from the highest levels of the Trump administration. \uc2 John Bolton\uc2 , a former U.S. ambassador to the U.N. who has pressed the White House to toughen its stance on Iran and North Korea, was tapped to succeed \uc2 Lt. Gen. H.R. McMaster\uc2  as national security adviser. "This change could make new sanctions on Iran more likely, which no doubt would further unsettle market participants and lend support in turn to gold," says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . (david.hodari@wsj.com; @davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 1408 GMT - Equity markets fall and the yen gains following U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's plans for tariffs aimed at China and the appointment of \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  as National Security Adviser, yet the typically risk-sensitive Australian dollar trades 0.4% higher against the U.S. dollar at 0.7722. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  often falls in a risk-averse market, but \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  strategist \uc2 Esther Reichelt\uc2  says it "has benefited a lot" from Australia's exemption from U.S. tariffs. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will suffer if the trade conflict escalates, given Australia's strong China links, but if the situation stays calm it may benefit from expectations that Australian interest rates could rise, Ms. Reichelt says. The New Zealand dollar also rises 0.6% against the U.S. dollar. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -0- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 23, 2018 10:54 ET (14:54 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180323ee3n002fe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pn