\par\par \b \uc2 JGB Yields Give Rise to  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Chatter -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,288 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2018\par 08:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0701 GMT - Yields on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark 10-year JGBs got up to 0.095% today, giving rise to market speculation that the BoJ could step in to tame rising yields. Some of selloff is in response to higher yields worldwide and some stems from ongoing declines in dollar-yen. As such, the yield has moved toward levels seen last year when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  acted to push JGB yields back down. The 10-year reached 0.15% on Feb. 3 and 0.105% on July 7, and they're seen as a "virtual limit," says Naka Matsuzawa, chief Japan rates strategist with \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2  . He'll be watching "what sort of measure they use and what kind of message they send" if another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurs because "this is a rather politically sensitive period" with Kuroda's term ending in April. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com; @SuryatapaB)\par \par \uc2 0700 GMT - The sentix Euro Break-up Index falls to a historic low of 6.9% in January, benefiting from the eurozone still being in robust condition, the publisher of the index says. According to sentix, "the focus will naturally be on Italy," as a potential problem area. Its upcoming elections on March 4 are a source of uncertainty, and the Italian sub-index is bucking the general trend, rising to 5.22%, sentix says. The headline, or main index, indicates that 6.9% of all surveyed investors expect the euro to break up within the next 12 months. The index reached its high at 73% in July 2012. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT - Singapore's projection of steady and solid investment from companies this year fits in with the city state's steady economic growth momentum. The Singapore Economic Development Boardpencils in S$8-S$10 billion in fixed asset investment in 2018, matching its target for last year. Actual investment in 2017 reached S$9.4 billion, according to the board's figures. The projections show global companies remain interested in Singapore as a strategic location for key business functions, it says. The economy is seen remaining largely stable despite potential hits from a possible goods and services tax increase and some cooling in global tech demand. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0522 GMT - Chinese property developer Yango is the 1st Asian issuer to postpone a dollar-bond offering this year, pulling a 3-year, $250 million B-rated offering last night on what it called weak market conditions. The company was offering 8.875%. The scotching happened as global bond yields rose further Monday, with 10-year Treasurys reaching their highest level in nearly 4 years. Even though there is a big pipeline of dollar deals from Asian issuers, none have launched today. (manju.dalal@wsj.com; @manjudalalsg)\par \par \uc2 0519 GMT - A weak La Nina weather pattern continues in the Pacific Ocean but may have peaked in recent weeks, says \uc2 Australia's weather bureau\uc2  . Sea-surface temperatures in the central tropical Pacific have warmed slightly since late December, the bureau notes in an update, with most models now forecasting that La Nina will end starting in March. La Ninas typically bring more rainfall in Southeast Asia and eastern Australia but trigger drier weather in southern Brazil, eastern Argentina and the southern US, potentially affecting production of grains and oilseeds. But Australia's update says the weakness of the current La Nina should mean little impact on rainfall levels. (rob.taylor@wsj.com; @WSJRobTaylor)\par \par \uc2 0358 GMT - An extension of a drought classification in New Zealand's South Island suggests the lengthy duration of farmers' struggles with especially dry weather may start to have a wider impact on the economy. The formal request for the classification, which triggers government funding, came from community groups and was made to New Zealand's agriculture minister this week, according to a statement. Economists say the dry weather will crimp milk production, making New Zealand's bumper monthly trade surplus unsustainable. Milk comes after tourism as New Zealand's main export. (ben.collins@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0347 GMT - While the Australian dollar has potential to climb further this year, multiple fault lines could change the outlook very quickly, says \uc2 Greg Gibbs\uc2 , strategist at Amp GFX. Global investor sentiment buoyed by more synchronized global growth and a greenback that is struggling to find its feet are among the factors that could keep the Aussie rising, Gibbs says. But above 0.8000 it starts to look fully priced, he says. The fault lines include potentially faster US rate hikes, rising global bond yields, more volatile global markets, Chinese credit tightening, a weaker Australian housing market, and the political cycle in Australia, Gibbs adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0341 GMT - Continuing patchiness in Japan's household spending that contrasts with strengthening retail sales points to the widening disparity in the economy, says Tetsuhide Mikamo, director of Marubeni Research Institute. "As the rich-poor gap widens, you get 2 pictures of Japan, where the rich are spending and everyone else is not," Mikamo says. Improved earnings at companies need to be translated into higher base wages to boost household spending, he says. If higher profits just lead to higher bonuses, people, anxious about the future, simply bank them as one-off payments. Household spending edged down 0.1% in December, while retail sales in 2017 grew at the fastest pace in 7 years. (mayumi.negishi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT - Japan's retail sales increased by the largest margin in 7 years in 2017, a sign that at least this element of domestic consumption is showing more of the momentum needed to fuel Japan's economic growth. Sales rose 1.9% in 2017, the biggest gain since a 2.6% increase in 2010. Contributing to the increase were auto sales, thanks to new models, petrochemical products, due to higher crude prices, and cosmetics sales, owing to demand from foreign travelers. The \uc2 Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry\uc2  slightly upgrades its view of retail sales, saying they are gradually picking up, compared with showing signs of pickup previously. Household spending figures released earlier in the day continue to show weakness.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT - Capacity utilization in the Australian economy rose strongly in December, according to the latest NAB Business Survey. It's a welcome result after several months of stagnation, says ANZ. In trend terms, the ongoing absorption of spare capacity bodes well for the labor market and suggests further improvements in the unemployment rate and, over time, wage growth is likely. Firms also reported profitability rose marginally in December to finish 2017 with the strongest annual average since 2007, the bank adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com ; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT - Aussie consumers are upbeat on the economy. \uc2 Craig James\uc2  , chief economist at Commsec says the outlook for the economy over the next year is the brightest in almost five years. Clearly the latest global forecasts from the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  have resonated with the Aussie consumers. And the strengthening of the job market hasn't been lost on people either, he adds. It bodes well for spending in 2018. (james.glynn@wsj.com ; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT - China set the yuan's daily fix weaker Tuesday following the greenback's gains overnight. The \uc2 PBoC\uc2  put today's daily midpoint at CNY6.3312, versus CNY6.3267 and yesterday's onshore close around CNY6.3327. The pair remains around the levels last seen before August 2015's devaluation of the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 30, 2018 02:01 ET (07:01 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180130ee1u000kq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
